const Map<String, StoryTranslation> enStoryTranslations = {
  // ===== Miracles =====
  'miracle-1': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Splitting of the Moon',
    body:
        'The chiefs of Quraysh gathered around the Prophet ﷺ in Makkah. Their arguing and denial had dragged on for a long time, and they said to him: "If you are truthful in your claim to prophethood, show us an extraordinary sign that no human being could perform." That night the Prophet ﷺ looked at the moon, which was full in the sky, and pointed to it with his finger. The moon split into two halves before their very eyes, so clearly that they could see Mount Hira standing out between them, and then it came back together as it had been. The Prophet ﷺ stood saying to them: "Bear witness, bear witness," so that none of them could claim afterward that he had seen nothing. But stubbornness had blinded their hearts. Instead of believing, their leaders told the people: "This is the magic of Ibn Abi Kabshah; he has bewitched you with it, so do not believe him." Some of the more sensible among them replied: "How could Muhammad alone bewitch all the people of Makkah, and all the people of the earth? Even if he has bewitched us, he cannot bewitch everyone everywhere." So some of them decided to test the matter another way. They waited for the caravans arriving from distant journeys, from Yemen and from Syria, and asked them: "Did you see anything strange in the sky that night?" All the travelers answered, without having agreed among themselves: "Yes, we saw the moon split into two pieces on that very night." This was decisive proof that what the people of Makkah had seen was neither imagination nor magic, but a real sign from Allah, about which Allah revealed His words: "The Hour has come near, and the moon has split. And if they see a sign, they turn away and say, \'Passing magic.\'"',
    source:
        'Sahih al-Bukhari and Muslim — in explanation of Surah Al-Qamar: 1–2',
  ),
  'miracle-2': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Night Journey and the Ascension',
    body:
        'That night was one of the most wondrous nights in the history of prophethood. The Prophet ﷺ was sleeping in his house in Makkah after a year of sorrow in which he had lost his uncle Abu Talib and his wife Khadijah. Jibril (Gabriel), peace be upon him, came to him with a white mount called Al-Buraq, swift as lightning. The Prophet ﷺ rode it, and it covered the distance between him and Al-Masjid Al-Aqsa in Jerusalem in moments that cannot be measured by ordinary time. On the way, the Prophet ﷺ stopped at places Jibril pointed out to him and prayed two rak\'ahs in each. They were places where earlier prophets had lived, such as Taybah, which would later become the home of his migration, and Bethlehem, the birthplace of Isa (Jesus), peace be upon him. When he reached Al-Masjid Al-Aqsa, he found the souls of all the prophets gathered there waiting for him, and the Prophet ﷺ led them in prayer, a sign that this religion is the seal of all the messages that came before it. Then he was taken up from the Rock of Al-Masjid Al-Aqsa to the lowest heaven, where he saw Adam, peace be upon him. Then he rose heaven after heaven, seeing Yahya (John) and Isa, Yusuf (Joseph), Idris (Enoch), Harun (Aaron), and Musa (Moses), until he reached the seventh heaven, where he saw Ibrahim (Abraham), peace be upon him, resting his back against Al-Bayt Al-Ma\'mur (the Frequented House). Then he was raised to a level that no angel brought near and no prophet sent before him had ever reached. There, fifty prayers a day were made obligatory upon him. He returned with them, passing by Musa, peace be upon him, who said to him: "Your nation will not be able to bear this, so go back to your Lord and ask Him to lighten it." The Prophet ﷺ went back again and again until they became five prayers equal in reward to fifty. In the morning he told Quraysh what he had seen. Most of them denied him and mocked him, and some of them went to Abu Bakr saying: "Look at what your companion is saying!" Abu Bakr said without hesitation: "If he said that, then he has spoken the truth. I believe him in things far beyond that — I believe him about news from heaven, morning and evening." From that day on he was called As-Siddiq (the Truthful).',
    source: 'Surah Al-Isra: 1 — Sahih al-Bukhari and Muslim',
  ),
  'miracle-3': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Water Flowing from Between His Fingers',
    body:
        'The Prophet ﷺ and his companions were on a journey when severe thirst overtook them, and they had only a little water in a small vessel, barely enough for one man to make wudu (ablution). The Prophet ﷺ called for that vessel and placed his blessed hand in it, and the companions saw something they had never seen before: water gushed from between his fingers ﷺ like springs, flowing abundantly without stopping. The whole army came forward to drink and make wudu until everyone had quenched their thirst and filled their containers, and they numbered about one thousand five hundred men. Jabir ibn Abdullah, who was one of those present, was asked how many had drunk, and he said: "Even if we had been a hundred thousand, it would have been enough for us. We were fifteen hundred." This was not a one-time event: water flowed from between his fingers ﷺ on more than one occasion, which made the companions certain that Allah brought about at his hands what no human being could do, to support him and to make firm the hearts of the believers who were with him.',
    source: 'Sahih al-Bukhari',
  ),
  'miracle-4': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Food That Glorified Allah in His Hands',
    body:
        'One of the companions invited the Prophet ﷺ and those with him to a meal at his house, but the food he had was only enough for a few people, while there were many more with the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ placed his hand on the food, mentioned the name of Allah, and asked for blessing in it. Then he allowed the people to eat ten at a time. Each group of ten ate until they were full and then left so that another ten could come, until everyone had eaten and was satisfied, and some of the food was still left over. Some of those present at that gathering reported that they used to hear coming from it — while it was in front of the Prophet ﷺ, before it was handed out to the people — a sound like the humming of bees or a quiet glorification of Allah. This is among the wonders with which Allah supported His Prophet ﷺ: a small amount of food in his hand would multiply through his blessing until it was enough for many times the number it could have fed before he touched it.',
    source: 'Sahih al-Bukhari',
  ),
  'miracle-5': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Staff of Musa Turns into a Snake',
    body:
        'Allah sent Musa (Moses), peace be upon him, to Pharaoh, who claimed to be a god and acted as a tyrant in the land. Musa asked his Lord for a proof with which he could establish the truth of his message before this tyrant. Allah commanded him to throw down his staff, the one he leaned on and used to bring down leaves for his sheep. He threw it down, and suddenly it was a huge snake moving swiftly, so that Musa became afraid of it and turned to flee. His Lord called to him: "Seize it and fear not; We will return it to its former condition." Then Musa put his hand into the opening of his garment and brought it out, and it was white, shining with light, without any illness or blemish. Musa went with these two signs to Pharaoh, but Pharaoh was arrogant and gathered his chief magicians to confront Musa on a great day when the people assembled. The magicians threw down their ropes and staffs, and because of the power of their magic it seemed to the onlookers that they were snakes moving about, so that Musa felt a moment of fear within himself. Allah revealed to him: "Fear not. Indeed, it is you who are superior. And throw what is in your right hand; it will swallow up what they have crafted." So Musa threw down his staff, and in moments it swallowed up all the illusions the magicians had made. The magicians themselves — who knew magic better than anyone — realized that this was no human magic, and they fell down in prostration, saying: "We have believed in the Lord of Harun (Aaron) and Musa," despite Pharaoh threatening to cut off their hands and feet on opposite sides.',
    source:
        'Surah Taha: 17–21, Surah Al-A\'raf: 107–122, and Surah Ash-Shu\'ara: 38–51',
  ),
  'miracle-6': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Cow of the Children of Israel and the Murdered Man Who Spoke',
    body:
        'A mysterious murder took place among the Children of Israel: a wealthy old man was killed at night, and no one knew for certain who had killed him. The people disagreed and quarreled, and the dispute nearly sparked strife between the tribes, each accusing the other of the killing. So they turned to their prophet Musa (Moses), peace be upon him, asking that Allah reveal the killer to them. Allah revealed to Musa that he should command them to slaughter a cow and then strike the dead man with part of it, so that he would come back to life by Allah\'s permission and tell them who had killed him. Here began the stubbornness for which the Children of Israel were known. Instead of hurrying to slaughter any cow as they had been commanded, they began making things difficult with their questions, thinking that this way they could escape the command. They said to Musa: "Call upon your Lord to make clear to us what it is." He answered them that it was a cow neither old — that is, not aged and worn out — nor virgin — that is, not so young that it had never given birth — but in between. Then they asked again, about its color, and they were told that it was a yellow cow, bright in color, pleasing to the observers. Still not satisfied, they asked a third time: "Indeed, all cows look alike to us. Call upon your Lord to make clear to us what it is. And indeed we, if Allah wills, will be guided." Then came the final description: a cow neither trained to plow the earth nor to irrigate the field, one free from fault with no spot upon it — that is, sound and without defects, never used for plowing or watering. With this precise description, they could find only one cow that matched it, belonging to a young man who looked after it, so they had to buy it at a very high price — it is said, its hide filled with gold — and they slaughtered it as they had been commanded. Then they struck the dead man with part of it, and Allah brought him back to life for a few moments by His permission, and he told them the name of his real killer: it was his nephew, who had killed him out of greed for his inheritance and so that he could marry his daughter — the murdered man\'s daughter — without waiting or asking permission. The killer had thought he would escape punishment for a crime with no witnesses, but Allah revealed the truth in a way that had never crossed his mind. The murdered man then returned to death, and the people knew the truth. In this story there is a great lesson: Allah is able to bring every hidden thing to light, and being excessively strict in religion — as the Children of Israel were with their many questions — only makes matters harder for oneself.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Baqarah: 67–73',
  ),
  'miracle-7': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Feeding the Army of the Trench from a Little Barley',
    body:
        'During the days of digging the Trench, hunger grew severe among the Muslims as they worked for days on end in the bitter cold, so much so that some of them tied a stone against their stomachs because of how hungry they were. Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, saw how exhausted the Prophet ﷺ was, so he asked his permission to go home, and he said to his wife: "Do you have anything? I have seen that the Prophet ﷺ is very hungry." She said: "I have a little barley and a young goat." Jabir slaughtered the young goat and his wife ground the barley, and he asked the Prophet ﷺ to come with just a few people, because the food was little and would not be enough for everyone. But the Prophet ﷺ called out loudly across the Trench: "O people of the Trench, Jabir has prepared a meal, so come along, all of you!" Jabir was astonished and hurried to his wife before they arrived, telling her that the Prophet ﷺ had invited all the people of the Trench, and they were a thousand men. She said: "Did he ask you how much we have?" Jabir realized how awkward his position was, but the Prophet ﷺ entered the house and ordered that the cooking pot not be taken off the fire and the dough not be baked until he came himself. Then he prayed for blessing and began breaking the bread and placing meat on it with his blessed hand, serving the people ten at a time: they would come in and eat until they were full, then leave, until all thousand men had eaten and left satisfied, while the pot was still boiling, as full as before, and the dough was still as it had been, through the blessing of his supplication ﷺ.',
    source: 'Sahih al-Bukhari and Muslim',
  ),
  'miracle-8': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Prophet\'s ﷺ Prayer for Rain',
    body:
        'A severe drought struck the people of Madinah in the time of the Prophet ﷺ. The rain was withheld, the land dried up, and the livestock perished, until a Bedouin man entered the mosque on Friday while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the sermon from the pulpit, and said: "O Messenger of Allah, our wealth has perished and our families are hungry, so ask Allah to send us rain." The Prophet ﷺ raised his blessed hands — and there was not a single wisp of cloud in the sky — and supplicated: "O Allah, send us rain! O Allah, send us rain! O Allah, send us rain!" Anas ibn Malik, the narrator of the hadith, said: "By Allah, the Prophet ﷺ had not yet come down from his pulpit before I saw the rain dripping down his blessed beard." The rain fell that day and continued for a whole week, until this time they feared flooding. So the same man — or someone else — came the following Friday and said: "O Messenger of Allah, the houses have collapsed and the roads are cut off, so ask Allah to hold it back from us." The Prophet ﷺ smiled, raised his hands, and said: "O Allah, around us and not upon us. O Allah, upon the hills and the high ground, the valley beds, and the places where trees grow." The clouds cleared away from Madinah and scattered around its outskirts, as if an unseen hand were parting them by Allah\'s command, while the city remained safe from harm in their midst. This is among the most wondrous times his supplication ﷺ was answered, so quickly and before everyone\'s eyes.',
    source: 'Sahih al-Bukhari and Muslim',
  ),
  'miracle-9': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Qarun Swallowed by the Earth',
    body:
        'Qarun (Korah) was from the people of Musa (Moses), peace be upon him, and Allah had given him so much wealth that the keys to his treasures alone would weigh down a band of strong men. His people advised him, saying: "Do not exult. Indeed, Allah does not like the exultant. But seek, through that which Allah has given you, the home of the Hereafter; and do not forget your share of the world. And do good as Allah has done good to you. And do not desire corruption in the land." But pride had blinded Qarun\'s insight, and he answered them with open arrogance: "I was only given it because of knowledge I have," denying Allah\'s favor upon him and claiming that everything he owned came from his own cleverness alone. One day he went out before his people in his dazzling adornment, with his splendid clothes and large entourage. Some of those who saw his glitter wished they had the like of the worldly fortune Qarun had been given, while the people of knowledge and faith among them said: "Woe to you! The reward of Allah is better for he who believes and does righteousness. And none are granted it except the patient." Allah did not leave him long in his arrogance and ingratitude: He caused the earth to swallow him and his home in a single moment, and there was for him no company to aid him other than Allah, nor was he of those who could defend themselves. Those who had wished for his position the day before began to say, regretful and having learned their lesson: "Oh, how Allah extends provision to whom He wills of His servants and restricts it! If not that Allah had conferred favor on us, He would have caused it to swallow us. Oh, how the disbelievers do not succeed!" They suddenly realized that the wealth for which they had envied him was not a blessing free of accountability, but a great test in which his arrogance and ingratitude destroyed him.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Qasas: 76–82',
  ),
  'miracle-10': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The People of the Sabbath Who Were Turned into Apes',
    body:
        'Allah tested a group of the Children of Israel who lived in a town by the seashore with a subtle trial: they were commanded not to catch fish on the Sabbath, their day set aside for worship and rest. Allah would show them the fish coming to them on the Sabbath in great numbers, clearly visible on the surface of the water, while on the other days of the week the fish disappeared completely — a test of how sincere their obedience was. But some of them could not patiently bear this temporary deprivation, so they turned to a cunning trick to get around the command without outwardly breaking it: on Friday they dug pools and set nets in which the fish would gather by themselves on the Sabbath, and then they would come and catch them right after the Sabbath ended, thinking that in this way they had not violated the explicit wording of the command, even though they had violated its purpose and spirit. The people of the town split into three groups: a group that carried out this forbidden trick; a group that kept away from them and forbade them from doing it; and a third group that stayed silent, neither doing it nor objecting to it, and instead said in surprise to those who were forbidding it: "Why do you advise a people whom Allah is about to destroy or to punish with a severe punishment? What is the use of advising them?" The advisers answered: "To be absolved before your Lord, and perhaps they may fear Him" — meaning that they gave advice to fulfill their duty, regardless of the outcome. When the wrongdoers arrogantly persisted in their scheming, Allah\'s humiliating punishment came upon them: they were transformed into apes, despised and cast out — a punishment fitting their crime of cunningly getting around Allah\'s command through trickery — so that it would be a lesson for those after them that getting around Allah\'s limits with outward cleverness does not save anyone from His punishment; rather, it may be the greater sin.',
    source: 'Surah Al-A\'raf: 163–166',
  ),
  // ===== Prophets =====
  'prophet-1': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Adam and the Beginning of Creation',
    body:
        'When Allah willed to create Adam, peace be upon him, He informed His angels, and they said in wonder: "Will You place upon it one who causes corruption therein and sheds blood, while we declare Your praise and sanctify You?" Their Lord answered them: "Indeed, I know that which you do not know." Allah created Adam from clay, breathed into him of the soul He created, and taught him the names of all things — knowledge that the angels did not have. Then He presented these things to the angels and asked them their names, but they could not answer. He said to them: "Did I not tell you that I know the unseen of the heavens and the earth?" Then He commanded the angels to prostrate to Adam, out of honor for him and not as worship. They all prostrated except Iblis (Satan), who was one of the jinn. He refused, was arrogant, and said: "I am better than him. You created me from fire and created him from clay." So he was cast out of Allah\'s mercy and cursed. He asked his Lord to give him respite until the Day of Resurrection so that he could lead the children of Adam astray, and Allah granted him respite as a test for people. Allah made Adam and his wife Hawwa (Eve) dwell in Paradise, and permitted them all its fruits except one tree, which He forbade them to approach. Iblis whispered to them and swore to them that he was a sincere adviser, and that it was the tree of eternal life, until they ate from it. Their private parts became apparent to them, and they felt shame for the first time. They turned to Allah in repentance and regret, so Allah accepted their repentance and taught them words with which to supplicate. Then they were sent down to the earth to be the first two human beings to live on it, and so began the story of all humankind.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Baqarah: 30–39 and Surah Al-A\'raf: 11–25',
  ),
  'prophet-2': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Nuh and the Ark',
    body:
        'Allah sent Nuh (Noah), peace be upon him, to a people who worshipped idols instead of Allah. He called them to the Oneness of Allah for nine hundred and fifty years, by night and by day, in private and in public, but his call only made them flee further and grow more stubborn. Their leaders would prevent the poor and the weak from sitting with him, and they mocked him whenever he passed by them, until Nuh complained to his Lord: "My Lord, indeed I invited my people night and day. But my invitation increased them not except in flight." Then Allah revealed to him that he should build a ship — a strange command in a land far from the sea. Nuh began building it amid the mockery of his people, who passed by him jeering: "Are you building a ship in the desert, Nuh?" He would reply to them: "If you ridicule us, then we will ridicule you just as you ridicule. And you are going to know." When the ship was complete, the sign that Allah had appointed came: the oven overflowed — that is, water gushed out abundantly from a place where bread was usually baked — and this announced the start of the great flood. Allah commanded Nuh to carry in the ship a pair of every kind of creature, male and female, along with those of his people who believed with him — and they were only a few — and his family, except those against whom the word had already gone forth. Nuh called out to his son, who had kept apart and did not board with them: "O my son, come aboard with us and be not with the disbelievers." He answered: "I will take refuge on a mountain to protect me from the water." Nuh said: "There is no protector today from the decree of Allah, except for whom He gives mercy." And the waves came between them, and he was among the drowned — even though he was the son of a prophet, because he did not follow his father\'s faith. The flood kept spreading over the earth until it covered the mountains, and everyone who disbelieved perished. Then Allah commanded the sky to withhold its rain and the earth to swallow its water, and the ship came to rest on Mount Judi, and it was said: "Away with the wrongdoing people."',
    source: 'Surah Hud: 25–49 and Surah Nuh',
  ),
  'prophet-3': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Ibrahim in the Fire',
    body:
        'Ibrahim (Abraham), peace be upon him, grew up among a people who worshipped idols. He began reflecting on the universe around him in search of the truth. He saw a star at night and said: "This is my lord." But when it set, he said: "I like not those that disappear." Then he saw the moon rising and said: "This is my lord." But when it set, he said: "Unless my Lord guides me, I will surely be among the people gone astray." Then he saw the sun rising and said: "This is my lord; this is greater." But when it set, he realized that all these created things set and pass away, and he said: "Indeed, I have turned my face toward He who created the heavens and the earth, inclining toward truth." Then he began to challenge his people over their idol worship. One day he entered their temple while they had gone out to one of their festivals, and with an axe in his hand he broke all the idols except the largest of them, and he hung the axe around its neck. When they returned and found their gods smashed, they asked: "Who has done this to our gods?" They were told: "We heard a young man mention them who is called Ibrahim." So they brought him and asked him: "Have you done this to our gods, O Ibrahim?" He answered them cleverly: "Rather, this — the largest of them — did it, so ask them, if they should be able to speak." For a moment they turned back to themselves and realized their foolishness, but pride stopped them from admitting it, and they said: "You have already known that these do not speak." He replied to them: "Then do you worship instead of Allah that which does not benefit you at all or harm you? Uff to you and to what you worship instead of Allah. Then will you not use reason?" They became angry and agreed on a harsh punishment: to burn him with fire. For days they gathered a great deal of firewood until it became an enormous fire that no one could come near, and because of the intensity of its flames they hurled him into it with a catapult. At that very moment, Allah commanded the fire directly: "O fire, be coolness and safety upon Ibrahim." It did not burn him; rather, he came out of it safe and sound, with no harm having touched him — it is even said that the only thing that burned was the rope that bound him. The people saw a great miracle before their eyes, yet most of them insisted on disbelief despite it.',
    source:
        'Surah Al-Anbiya: 51–70, Surah Al-An\'am: 74–79, and Surah As-Saffat: 83–98',
  ),
  'prophet-4': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Yusuf and His Brothers',
    body:
        'When Yusuf (Joseph), peace be upon him, was a young boy, he saw a wondrous dream: eleven stars and the sun and the moon, which he saw prostrating to him. He told it to his father Ya\'qub (Jacob), peace be upon him, and Ya\'qub understood that his son would reach a great station, so he warned him: "O my son, do not relate your vision to your brothers, or they will contrive against you a plan." But the envy of his brothers — for he was the dearest of his sons to his father\'s heart — went beyond all limits. They agreed to get rid of him, persuaded their father to send him with them to play, and then threw him into the bottom of a well, an abandoned well in the desert, leaving him alone and afraid. They took his shirt, stained it with false blood, and returned to their father weeping, claiming that a wolf had eaten him. Ya\'qub grieved so deeply that his eyes turned white from sorrow, yet he never lost hope in Allah\'s mercy. At the well, a caravan passed by looking for water. They let down their bucket and Yusuf clung to it, and they rejoiced at what they had found and sold him in Egypt for a paltry price to a man called Al-Aziz. Yusuf grew up in the house of Al-Aziz, and he faced a great trial when the wife of Al-Aziz tried to seduce him. He said: "I seek the refuge of Allah. Indeed, he is my master, who has made good my residence," and he held firm despite her temptation. He chose prison over disobedience and spent years there unjustly, but he used that time to interpret the dreams of the prisoners, until word of him reached the king when the king saw a dream that baffled his interpreters: seven fat cows being eaten by seven lean ones. Yusuf interpreted it as years of plenty followed by years of drought, and advised how the harvests should be managed. The king admired his wisdom, released him from prison, and put him in charge of the storehouses of the land. When the years of famine came, his brothers came to Egypt seeking food. Yusuf recognized them, but they did not recognize him. He gently arranged matters until his youngest brother Binyamin (Benjamin) was brought, and in the end he revealed himself to them. They trembled with shame and regret, and he said to them the words the Qur\'an has immortalized: "No blame will there be upon you today. Allah will forgive you; and He is the most merciful of the merciful." He sent his shirt to his father, and his sight returned to him, and the family was reunited after a separation that had lasted many long years.',
    source: 'Surah Yusuf',
  ),
  'prophet-5': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Musa and the Exodus of the Children of Israel',
    body:
        'Pharaoh enslaved the Children of Israel in Egypt for centuries, slaughtering their sons and keeping their women alive, out of fear of a prophecy that said a boy from among them would bring about the end of his kingdom. Musa (Moses) was born during that time, and Allah inspired his mother to place him in a chest and cast it into the river when she feared for him. The water carried him until he reached Pharaoh\'s own palace, where Pharaoh\'s wife Asiya picked him up and loved him dearly, insisting on raising him despite Pharaoh\'s initial opposition. It was decreed that Musa would be nursed by his real mother without anyone knowing, for he refused the breast of every other wet nurse until they brought his mother, and he accepted her. Allah did this out of mercy to her after she had been forced to part from him. Musa grew up in Pharaoh\'s own house, and when he became a young man, he accidentally killed an Egyptian man while defending a man from his own people, so he fled in fear to Madyan (Midian). There he married and worked as a shepherd for many years. On his way back, he saw a fire near Mount Tur (Sinai), and he went to bring a flame from it. There his Lord called to him directly and commanded him to go to Pharaoh, who had transgressed and claimed to be a god, and gave him the two signs of the staff and the white hand. Musa went with his brother Harun (Aaron) to Pharaoh, calling him to worship Allah and to let the Children of Israel go. Pharaoh was arrogant and challenged him with his magicians, as told before; when the magicians believed, Pharaoh threatened them, but they held firm to their faith. Pharaoh continued in his tyranny despite the signs that came one after another — the flood, the locusts, the lice, the frogs, and the blood — until Allah permitted Musa to leave by night with the Children of Israel. Pharaoh pursued them with his army until he caught up with them at the sea. Musa\'s companions said: "Indeed, we are to be overtaken!" Musa answered them with great confidence: "No! Indeed, with me is my Lord; He will guide me." Allah revealed to him to strike the sea with his staff, and it split into twelve dry paths, with the water between them like great mountains, and the Children of Israel all crossed safely. When Pharaoh and his soldiers tried to follow them along the same path, Allah closed the sea over them and drowned them all before the eyes of the Children of Israel, who watched the end of the tyrant who had enslaved them for centuries come in a single moment.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Qasas: 7–43, Surah Ash-Shu\'ara: 10–68, and Surah Taha',
  ),
  'prophet-6': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Yunus and the Whale',
    body:
        'Yunus (Jonah), peace be upon him, was sent to the people of Nineveh to call them to the Oneness of Allah. They persisted in disbelief generation after generation until he grew weary of them. He warned them of a punishment close at hand if they did not believe, and then he left them in anger before his Lord had given him permission to depart — something for which Allah later reproached him. Yunus boarded a ship with other people, and when they reached the middle of the sea the waves rose and the danger grew until the ship nearly sank with everyone on board. The passengers decided to draw lots to choose which of them would be thrown into the sea to lighten the load, and the lot fell on Yunus. He was thrown into the raging sea, and a huge whale swallowed him by the command of Allah, who commanded it not to eat his flesh or break his bones, but to be a temporary prison for him. Yunus found himself in three darknesses: the darkness of the whale\'s belly, the darkness of the sea, and the darkness of the night. He realized his mistake in leaving his people without permission, and he began glorifying his Lord and seeking His forgiveness with the supplication the Qur\'an has immortalized: "There is no deity except You; exalted are You. Indeed, I have been of the wrongdoers." Allah answered him at once and commanded the whale to cast him out onto the shore. Yunus came out weak in body, like a newborn baby, and Allah caused a gourd plant to grow over him to shade him and protect him from the heat of the sun until he regained his strength. As for his people, whom he had left in anger, they saw the signs of the promised punishment drawing near, and they all repented to Allah at the last moment, so Allah lifted the punishment from them. They were the only people in history who all believed after seeing the signs of punishment and whose faith benefited them, and this is what the Qur\'an refers to in its words: "Then has there not been a single city that believed so its faith benefited it except the people of Yunus?"',
    source:
        'Surah As-Saffat: 139–148, Surah Al-Anbiya: 87–88, and Surah Yunus: 98',
  ),
  'prophet-7': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Ayyub and His Patience in Affliction',
    body:
        'Ayyub (Job), peace be upon him, was a prophet with much wealth and many children, grateful to his Lord for all His blessings. Then Allah tested him with various kinds of affliction to try his patience: all his wealth was lost, all his children died, and then a severe illness struck his body until his skin was covered in sores and everyone around him turned away from him — except his wife, who remained loyal, caring for him for many long years despite the severity of the trial. Yet through it all, his tongue never tired of remembering Allah, nor did he complain about his Lord; rather, he remembered Allah in every state and praised Him for what He had given before He tested him. The years of illness dragged on — it is said they lasted eighteen years — and in the end, when the harm became more than he could bear, he raised his hands to his Lord with the good manners of one who supplicates, not the despair of one who complains, and said: "Indeed, adversity has touched me, and You are the most merciful of the merciful." He did not openly ask for the affliction to be lifted; he simply laid his condition before his Lord. Allah answered him at once and commanded him to strike the ground with his foot. Cool water sprang up from beneath it; he drank from it and washed with it, and every illness and ailment left him. Allah gave him back his family and the like of them along with them, as a mercy from Him, and restored his health and youth as if nothing had happened. Ayyub\'s patience has become proverbial in every language to this day — people speak of "the patience of Ayyub" — showing that beautiful patience together with contentment is the path to relief, however long the trial lasts.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Anbiya: 83–84 and Surah Sad: 41–44',
  ),
  'prophet-8': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Dawud, Sulayman and the Case of the Field',
    body:
        'Two men came to Dawud (David), peace be upon him, seeking his judgment. One of them owned a field of crops, and the other owned sheep. The sheep of one had wandered at night into the other\'s field and destroyed all of it without his knowing. Dawud, peace be upon him, ruled that all the sheep should be given to the owner of the field as compensation for his loss, and on the face of it this was a just ruling. But his son Sulayman (Solomon), who was still young, offered his father another view that was kinder to both sides: the owner of the field would take the sheep for a limited time to benefit from their milk, wool, and offspring, while the owner of the sheep would take charge of farming the land and restoring it until it returned to how it had been, or better. Once the field was restored, the sheep would be returned to their owner and the field to its owner, so that no one would suffer a lasting loss. Dawud, peace be upon him, admired his son\'s profound wisdom despite his young age, and approved his ruling, because it was kinder to the people and more precise in achieving complete justice. Allah praised both of them together in the Qur\'an, saying: "And We gave understanding of the case to Sulayman, and to each of them We gave judgment and knowledge," to show that reasoned judgments may differ even among the wise themselves, and that Allah guides whom He wills to what is more precise and more gentle, without this taking anything away from the other.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Anbiya: 78–79',
  ),
  'prophet-9': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Sulayman, the Hoopoe, and the Queen of Sheba',
    body:
        'Allah gave Sulayman (Solomon), peace be upon him, a mighty kingdom. He placed the wind, the jinn, and the birds at his service, and taught him the speech of animals. Sulayman used to inspect his troops and keep count of them. One day he inspected the birds and did not find the hoopoe among them, so he warned that he would punish it severely, or even slaughter it, unless it came with a clear excuse. Before long, the hoopoe arrived with momentous news that changed the course of the story: "I found a woman ruling over a people, and she has been given of everything, and she has a great throne. But she and her people prostrate to the sun instead of Allah, and Satan has made their deeds seem pleasing to them and turned them away from the right path." Sulayman did not punish the hoopoe; instead, he set out to test the truth of its report. He wrote a letter to that queen, Bilqis, the Queen of Sheba, and sent it with the hoopoe to deliver to her. Bilqis read the letter before the nobles of her people and said: "Indeed, it is from Sulayman, and indeed, it reads: \'In the name of Allah, the Entirely Merciful, the Especially Merciful. Be not haughty with me but come to me in submission.\'" She consulted her people, and they advised her to use force, for they were a people of great might. But her wisdom led her to choose prudence over confrontation, so she sent Sulayman a gift to test his intentions. Sulayman sent it back, saying that what Allah had given him was better than what He had given her, and he warned her of an army she could not withstand if she did not come in submission. Bilqis decided to go to him herself. Sulayman asked a powerful one (\'ifrit) from among the jinn to bring her throne before she arrived. The jinn said: "I will bring it to you before you rise from your place." But the one who had knowledge from the Scripture said: "I will bring it to you before your glance returns to you." And so it was, by Allah\'s permission. When Bilqis arrived and saw her throne before her, slightly altered to test her, she recognized it. And when she entered a palace made of glass, she thought it was a pool of water and uncovered her shins. Sulayman told her that it was a palace made smooth with glass. She then realized the greatness of his kingdom and said: "My Lord, indeed I have wronged myself, and I submit with Sulayman to Allah, Lord of the worlds."',
    source: 'Surah An-Naml: 20–44',
  ),
  'prophet-10': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Miraculous Birth of Isa',
    body:
        'Maryam (Mary), the daughter of Imran, was a devout young woman who withdrew from her family to devote herself to worship in a prayer chamber of her own. Jibril (Gabriel), peace be upon him, came to her in the form of a well-proportioned man. She was frightened of him and said: "Indeed, I seek refuge in the Most Merciful from you, if you should be fearing of Allah." He reassured her: "I am only the messenger of your Lord to give you [news of] a pure boy." Maryam was utterly astonished and said: "How can I have a boy while no man has touched me and I have not been unchaste?" He answered: "Thus [it will be]; your Lord says, \'It is easy for Me, and We will make him a sign to the people and a mercy from Us.\'" So Maryam conceived by the command of Allah alone, without a father. She withdrew from her people to a remote place, fearing the gossip of others. The pains of childbirth drove her to the trunk of an old palm tree, and in the depth of her pain and loneliness she said: "Oh, I wish I had died before this and was in oblivion, forgotten." Then Jibril called to her from below her, or it was the newborn himself who called: "Do not grieve; your Lord has provided beneath you a stream. And shake toward you the trunk of the palm tree; it will drop upon you ripe, fresh dates." Thus Allah honored her with this provision in her solitude. Maryam carried her baby to her people. When they saw her, they were outraged and accused her of indecency without hearing a single word from her. She pointed to the child so that they would speak to him, and they said in amazement: "How can we speak to one who is in the cradle a child?" Then Isa (Jesus) spoke, in yet another miracle, while he was a nursing infant not yet old enough to talk, and said: "Indeed, I am the servant of Allah. He has given me the Scripture and made me a prophet. And He has made me blessed wherever I am and has enjoined upon me prayer and zakah as long as I remain alive, and [made me] dutiful to my mother, and He has not made me a wretched tyrant." Everyone was stunned, and they recognized Maryam\'s innocence through the miracle of her son, who spoke to them from his cradle.',
    source: 'Surah Maryam: 16–33 and Surah Al Imran: 45–47',
  ),
  'prophet-11': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Musa\'s Mother Casts Her Baby into the River',
    body:
        'At a time when Pharaoh was slaughtering every newborn boy of the Children of Israel, fearing a prophecy that a boy from among them would bring about his downfall, the mother of Musa (Moses) gave birth to her son at the most dangerous of times. Her heart was filled with terror for him because of Pharaoh\'s spies, who were searching every house. Then Allah inspired her (an inspiration, not prophethood) with a command that at first seemed even more dangerous than keeping him at home: "Suckle him; but when you fear for him, cast him into the river and do not fear and do not grieve. Indeed, We will return him to you and will make him [one] of the messengers." It was an immense test of a mother\'s faith, that she should place her nursing baby with her own hands in the flowing river. Yet she obeyed with remarkable trust. She placed him in a tightly sealed chest and cast it into the waters of the Nile. She told her daughter, Musa\'s sister, to follow the chest from a distance along the riverbank and see where it came to rest, without anyone noticing her. The waves carried the chest until it reached Pharaoh\'s own palace, where the people of the palace picked it up. Pharaoh\'s wife, Asiya, loved the baby deeply from the very first glance and insisted on adopting him, despite Pharaoh\'s wish to kill him. Here the second miracle took place: Allah made the little baby refuse the breast of every wet nurse brought to him. He turned them all away no matter how hard they tried, until the people of the palace grew worried about his hunger. At that moment his sister, who had been watching from afar, stepped forward and cleverly offered: "Shall I direct you to a household that will be responsible for him for you while they are to him sincere?" Not suspecting her intentions, they agreed, and she brought his real mother. The baby took her breast at once, without hesitation. So Allah returned Musa to his mother\'s arms, to nurse him in Pharaoh\'s very own palace, for a wage paid from the treasury of the very man who had wanted to kill her son. This fulfilled the promise with which Allah had reassured her: that she might be content and not grieve, and that she would know that the promise of Allah is true. But most of the people do not know.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Qasas: 7–13',
  ),
  'prophet-12': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Asiya, the Wife of Pharaoh',
    body:
        'Asiya bint Muzahim was the wife of Pharaoh himself. She lived in the grandest and most luxurious palaces on earth, but her heart was not like the heart of her tyrant husband. When her servants found a chest floating on the waters of the Nile with a beautiful baby inside, she loved him instantly with an overwhelming love, and she pleaded with Pharaoh not to kill him, in words the Qur\'an has preserved forever: "[He will be] a comfort of the eye for me and for you. Do not kill him; perhaps he may benefit us, or we may adopt him as a son." Pharaoh reluctantly agreed, not realizing that he was raising in his own home the one who would bring about his destruction. As the years passed and Asiya saw with her own eyes the miracles of Musa (Moses) and the truth of his message, her heart secretly came to believe in the Lord of Musa and Harun (Aaron) alone, while her husband still claimed divinity for himself. She hid her faith for many long years, fearing her husband\'s cruelty, but in the end it was discovered. Pharaoh became furious with his own wife and decided to punish her with a punishment that, in his twisted mind, fit the "crime" of believing in a god other than him: he ordered that her hands and feet be stretched out and pinned to the ground with stakes under the scorching desert sun, and a massive, heavy rock was placed on her to increase her torment. In those final moments of her life, between the pain of her body and the firmness of her faith, Asiya lifted her heart to her Lord with a remarkable supplication. She did not ask for the worldly torment to be lifted; rather, she asked for something far greater: "My Lord, build for me near You a house in Paradise and save me from Pharaoh and his deeds and save me from the wrongdoing people." Allah answered her prayer and showed her her house in Paradise before she departed this life, so all the pains of this world became light for her as she saw the fruit of her patience with her own eyes. The Prophet ﷺ mentioned her among the very best women of all the worlds, together with Maryam (Mary) the daughter of Imran, Khadijah, and Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with them all.',
    source: 'Surah At-Tahrim: 11 and Surah Al-Qasas: 9 — Sahih al-Bukhari',
  ),
  'prophet-13': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Musa and Al-Khidr',
    body:
        'One day Musa (Moses), peace be upon him, stood addressing the Children of Israel, and he was asked: "Who is the most knowledgeable person on earth?" He said: "I am," believing that his knowledge through revelation made him so. Allah admonished him for not attributing knowledge to Him, glorified is He, and told him that He had a servant at the meeting point of the two seas who was more knowledgeable than him in certain matters. Musa asked to meet him so that he could learn from him, and the sign of the meeting was that he would lose a salted fish he was carrying in his bag, when it came back to life and leapt into the sea. And indeed, Musa and his young companion forgot the fish by the rock, so they went back, retracing their footsteps, until they found Al-Khidr. Musa politely asked to accompany him so that he could learn from what Allah had taught him. Al-Khidr set him a clear condition: "Indeed, with me you will never be able to have patience. And how can you have patience for what you do not encompass in knowledge?" Musa promised to be patient and not to ask him about anything until Al-Khidr himself mentioned it to him. They boarded a ship whose owners carried them free of charge, and suddenly Al-Khidr made a hole in it! Despite his promise, Musa could not contain himself and said: "Have you torn it open to drown its people? You have certainly done a grave thing." Al-Khidr reminded him of his first condition. Then they met a young boy, and Al-Khidr killed him for no apparent reason. Musa cried out in protest for the second time: "Have you killed a pure soul for other than [having killed] a soul? You have certainly done a deplorable thing." Al-Khidr said to him: "Did I not tell you that with me you would never be able to have patience?" They agreed that this would be the last chance. In a third town, they asked its people for food, but they refused to feed them despite their great hunger. Even so, they found there a wall about to collapse, and Al-Khidr set it straight without asking for any payment! So Musa questioned him for the third time: "If you wished, you could have taken for it a payment." Al-Khidr said: "This is parting between me and you," and he explained everything to him. The ship belonged to poor people who worked at sea, so he damaged it to make it look defective, so that a tyrant king who was seizing every sound ship would not take it. As for the boy, his parents were believers, and had he grown up he would have overburdened them with transgression and disbelief, so Allah willed to give them in exchange a child better than him, as a mercy, and more dutiful. And as for the wall, beneath it was a treasure belonging to two young orphans in the city, and their father had been a righteous man. So your Lord intended that they reach maturity and extract their treasure, as a mercy from your Lord. "And I did it not of my own accord," meaning by revelation from Allah, not by his own personal judgment.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Kahf: 60–82',
  ),
  'prophet-14': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Sacrifice of Ismail and the Great Ransom',
    body:
        'Ibrahim (Abraham), peace be upon him, saw in a dream that he was sacrificing his son Ismail (Ishmael). The dreams of prophets are true revelation, and there can be no hesitation about them. Ibrahim did not impose his decision on his son; instead, he put the matter to him with the gentleness of a loving father: "O my son, indeed I have seen in a dream that I [must] sacrifice you, so see what you think." Ismail was then a young boy who had reached the age of working alongside his father, and he answered with faith beyond his years: "O my father, do as you are commanded. You will find me, if Allah wills, of the steadfast." Satan tried to turn them both away from obeying this difficult command. He appeared to Ibrahim at three places, whispering to him not to do it, and each time Ibrahim pelted him with stones, firmly rejecting his whispers. These three places are the origin of the stoning of the Jamarat in the rites of Hajj to this day. When father and son had fully submitted to Allah\'s command, and Ibrahim had laid his son down on his forehead, ready to carry out the command with his own hand, the divine call came at the decisive moment: "O Ibrahim, you have fulfilled the vision. Indeed, We thus reward the doers of good. Indeed, this was the clear trial." What was truly required was not the shedding of blood; the test lay in the sincerity of submission and the complete obedience that father and son showed together without hesitation. Allah ransomed him with a great sacrifice, a ram sent down from Paradise through Jibril (Gabriel), peace be upon him, which was sacrificed in Ismail\'s place. Allah preserved this moment as an everlasting memory that Muslims revive every year on Eid al-Adha, when they sacrifice livestock in following this great ransom and in commemoration of this rare submission to the command of Allah.',
    source: 'Surah As-Saffat: 100–111',
  ),
  'prophet-15': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Lut and His People',
    body:
        'Lut (Lot), peace be upon him, was sent to a people who had invented an immorality that no one in all the worlds had committed before them. He called on them again and again to abandon this evil, but their only answer was to threaten to drive him and his family out of their town, saying: "Expel the family of Lut from your city. Indeed, they are people who keep themselves pure," mocking them because they wanted chastity and purity! No one in his town believed in Lut except his own household, and even then not his wife, who supported her people in what they did, and so she was among those destroyed with them. Allah sent His angels in the form of handsome young men. They first passed by Ibrahim (Abraham), peace be upon him, bringing him the good news of a learned son, and then continued on their way to Lut. When Lut saw them, he was greatly distressed on their account, fearing for them from his people, who had no mercy on a handsome guest, and he said: "This is a trying day." Indeed, as soon as the townspeople heard that Lut had guests, they came rushing to him, demanding them with disgusting insistence. Lut tried to hold them back by every possible means, until he said in sorrow: "If only I had against you some power or could take refuge in a strong support." At that point the angels revealed their true nature to reassure Lut, and in a single moment they blotted out the eyes of the aggressors so that they could no longer see anything. They commanded Lut to leave with his family in the depth of the night and that none of them should look back, except his wife, for she would be struck by what struck her people. In the morning, Allah\'s decisive command came: Allah made the highest part of the town its lowest, turning it upside down, and rained upon it stones of hard clay, marked, each one marked for a particular person among them. It was utterly destroyed, with no trace of it left, as a lesson for those who discern and reflect on the consequences of immorality.',
    source:
        'Surah Hud: 77–83, Surah Al-Hijr: 57–77, and Surah Al-A\'raf: 80–84',
  ),
  'prophet-16': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Shu\'ayb and the People of Madyan',
    body:
        'Shu\'ayb, peace be upon him, was sent to the people of Madyan (Midian). They were a people who cheated with scales and measures in their trade, cutting short what was due to others through a small trick in every deal, thinking it would go unnoticed. They also robbed travelers on the roads and spread corruption in the land. Shu\'ayb called them first to worship Allah alone, and then to honesty in trade: "O my people, give full measure and weight in justice and do not deprive the people of their due and do not commit abuse on the earth, spreading corruption." They answered him with astonishing ridicule and mockery: "Does your prayer command you that we should leave what our fathers worship or not do with our wealth what we please? Indeed, you are the forbearing, the discerning!" They said this in sarcasm, not in praise. They threatened him and those who believed with him with stoning, or with expulsion from the town, unless they returned to their religion. Shu\'ayb answered them with composure: "Even if we were unwilling? We would have invented against Allah a lie if we returned to your religion after Allah had saved us from it." He reminded them of the fate of the nations destroyed before them because of their tyranny, the peoples of Nuh (Noah), Hud, Salih, and Lut (Lot), and that those nations were not far from them in place or in time, and he called them to seek forgiveness and repent before it was too late. But their arrogance kept them from listening, so the punishment came to them in two forms mentioned in the Qur\'an: a mighty earthquake that shook the ground beneath them, and a canopy of punishment that closed over them, the punishment of a dark and terrible day. They lay fallen prone in their homes, dead and motionless, as though they had never lived there. After their destruction, Shu\'ayb stood addressing their empty homes with the sorrow of a prophet who had lost his people despite all they had done to him: "O my people, I had certainly conveyed to you the messages of my Lord and advised you, so how could I grieve for a disbelieving people?"',
    source:
        'Surah Hud: 84–95, Surah Al-A\'raf: 85–93, and Surah Ash-Shu\'ara: 176–191',
  ),
  'prophet-17': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Talut, Jalut, and the Young Dawud',
    body:
        'Long after the time of Musa (Moses), peace be upon him, the chiefs of the Children of Israel asked their prophet to appoint a king for them, under whose banner they would fight their enemies, who had driven them from their homes and taken their children captive. Their prophet warned them: "Would you perhaps refrain from fighting if battle was prescribed for you?" But they insisted, confident in themselves. So Allah chose Talut (Saul) as king for them. Many of them objected, because he was not from a royal family nor among the wealthiest of them: "How can he have kingship over us while we are more worthy of kingship than him and he has not been given any measure of wealth?" Their prophet told them that Allah had chosen him over them and had increased him abundantly in knowledge and stature, and He gave them a sign of the truth of his kingship: the return of the chest that had once been with the Children of Israel, in which was tranquility from their Lord and a remnant of what the family of Musa and the family of Harun (Aaron) had left behind, carried by the angels. Talut set out with his army and gave them a decisive test at a river: "Whoever drinks from it is not of me, and whoever does not taste it is indeed of me, excepting one who takes [from it] in the hollow of his hand." Most of the army drank without restraint despite the warning, and only a few of them passed the test. When this small number faced the vast army of Jalut (Goliath), some of them drew back in fear, but the steadfast believers among them spoke words that live on forever: "How many a small company has overcome a large company by permission of Allah. And Allah is with the patient." In Talut\'s ranks was a young man, not yet known for fighting, named Dawud (David). He stepped forward with remarkable confidence to face in single combat the mighty and terrifying Jalut, who had struck fear into the whole army. He struck him with his sling, a fatal blow that brought him down dead, and Jalut\'s army suffered a crushing defeat. After that, Allah gave Dawud kingship and wisdom and taught him from that which He willed, and he became one of the greatest prophets of the Children of Israel, after having been an unknown young man in the back ranks.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Baqarah: 246–251',
  ),
  'prophet-18': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Dhul-Qarnayn and the Wall Against Ya\'juj and Ma\'juj',
    body:
        'Dhul-Qarnayn was a righteous and just king whom Allah established in the land and to whom He gave a way to everything, meaning a means by which to reach his goal. He set out on great journeys. First he reached the setting of the sun, and found it, as it appeared to his eyes, setting in a spring of dark, muddy water, and near it he found a people. Allah gave him the choice regarding them: either to punish the wrongdoers among them or to treat them with kindness. He chose justice: "As for one who wrongs, we will punish him. But as for one who believes and does righteousness, he will have the best reward." Then he traveled in another direction until he reached the rising of the sun, and he found a people for whom Allah had not made any shield against it; they lived exposed to its direct heat with hardly any protection. He treated them, too, with the same justice. Then he took a third route until he reached a place between two mighty mountains, where he found a people who could hardly understand speech. They were worn out by the harm of Ya\'juj and Ma\'juj (Gog and Magog), two corrupt tribes who kept raiding them and ruining their crops and homes. They asked Dhul-Qarnayn to build them a barrier to protect them from those tribes, in return for wealth they would collect for him. He refused the payment with the dignity of a righteous king: "That in which my Lord has established me is better, but assist me with strength; I will make between you and them a dam." He asked them for blocks of iron and built with them between the two mountains, until, when he had leveled it between the two cliffs, the two sides of the mountains, he ordered them to blow on it with bellows until the iron became like glowing fire. Then he poured molten copper over it, which fused with it and hardened into an impregnable barrier that they could neither climb, because of its height and smoothness, nor tunnel through, because of its immense strength. Dhul-Qarnayn did not attribute this great feat of engineering to himself and his own cleverness. Instead he said, with the humility of a sincere believer: "This is a mercy from my Lord; but when the promise of my Lord comes, He will make it level, and ever is the promise of my Lord true." He was declaring that this barrier, however strong, would not last forever, but would be leveled at its appointed time, when the Hour draws near.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Kahf: 83–98',
  ),
  'prophet-19': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Habib the Carpenter, the Believer of the Town',
    body:
        'Allah sent two messengers to a town, but its people denied them, so He strengthened them with a third messenger. They said to the people of the town: "Indeed, we are messengers to you." But the people answered with contempt: "You are not but human beings like us, and the Most Merciful has not revealed a thing. You are only telling lies." They regarded them as a bad omen and threatened to stone them if they did not stop their call. The messengers replied steadfastly: "Your omen is with yourselves," meaning that your misfortune comes from yourselves, not from us. At that critical moment, a man came running from the farthest end of the city. The Qur\'an does not name him explicitly, but the scholars of tafsir called him Habib the Carpenter. He called out to his people with the voice of a sincere and trustworthy adviser: "O my people, follow the messengers. Follow those who do not ask of you any payment, and they are rightly guided." Then he declared his own faith openly before everyone, without fear of what his words would bring upon him: "Indeed, I have believed in your Lord, so listen to me!" His people could not bear this open challenge to their beliefs, so they fell upon him and killed him on the spot, without mercy, simply for declaring his faith. But Allah honored him immediately and wondrously: it was said to him, "Enter Paradise," and he entered it as a martyr at the very moment he departed this life. Even amid that great blessing, Habib did not forget the people who had killed him; rather, he wished them good even after his death: "I wish my people could know of how my Lord has forgiven me and placed me among the honored." As for the town that rejected the messengers and killed its only believer, Allah did not send down an army or any elaborate punishment upon it; a single shout was enough: "And We did not send down upon his people after him any soldiers from the heaven, nor would We have done so. It was not but one shout, and immediately they were extinguished." This shows that when Allah decrees that His punishment should descend, it needs no more than a single moment.',
    source: 'Surah Ya-Sin: 13–29',
  ),
  'prophet-20': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Garden Owners Who Withheld from the Poor',
    body:
        'A righteous man owned a large orchard with abundant fruit. It was his good custom that whenever harvest time came, he would leave a generous share of it for the poor and needy before gathering the rest for his family. This man passed away, and his sons inherited the orchard after him. But unlike their father, they secretly plotted among themselves to deprive the poor this time. They pledged to go out at dawn before anyone woke up, to gather all the fruit for themselves without any needy person noticing them and asking for his usual share. They swore to this with firm resolve, without any of them saying "if Allah wills." That very night, while they slept, confident in their plan, Allah sent upon the orchard a sudden affliction from Himself, a storm or a fire, that wiped it out completely within a few hours. It became like black, scorched, barren ground, with no fruit in it and no trace of life. The brothers woke up early as they had agreed and set out, whispering to one another: "Go early to your crop if you would cut the fruit. There will surely not enter upon you today any poor person." And they went early, in determination, thinking themselves able, that is, firmly resolved to keep the poor away. But when they arrived and saw the complete ruin, they thought for a moment that they had lost their way: they said, "Indeed, we are lost!" Then they realized the painful truth: "Rather, we have been deprived." They had been punished by losing all the fruit themselves because of their wicked intention to deprive others. The most fair-minded of them said, reproaching them: "Did I not say to you, \'Why do you not exalt Allah?\'" So they all repented in regret: "Exalted is our Lord! Indeed, we were wrongdoers." And they began blaming one another for their sinful plan, hoping that their Lord would give them something better in its place if they were sincere in their repentance.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Qalam: 17–33',
  ),
  'prophet-21': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Zakariyya and Yahya',
    body:
        'Zakariyya (Zechariah), peace be upon him, was an elderly prophet whose wife was barren and had never borne a child in all her life, so he had no one to inherit his prophethood and carry his message after him. He used to visit Maryam (Mary) in the prayer chamber where she had worshipped since childhood, and every time he entered upon her, he found with her provision of fruit out of season. He asked her in amazement: "O Maryam, from where is this coming to you?" She answered with the certainty of one who trusts her Lord: "It is from Allah. Indeed, Allah provides for whom He wills without account." This scene stirred new hope in Zakariyya\'s heart in Allah\'s power over all things, so at that moment he called upon his Lord with quiet humility, without raising his voice before people: "My Lord, indeed my bones have weakened, and my head has filled with white, and never have I been in my supplication to You, my Lord, unhappy. And indeed, I fear the successors after me, and my wife has been barren, so give me from Yourself an heir who will inherit me and inherit from the family of Ya\'qub (Jacob). And make him, my Lord, pleasing to You." Allah answered his prayer at once with wonderful news: "O Zakariyya, indeed We give you good tidings of a boy whose name will be Yahya (John). We have not assigned to any before this name," meaning no one had been given this name before him, as an honor and a distinction for him. Zakariyya was amazed at this news, despite his certainty in Allah\'s power: "My Lord, how will I have a boy when my wife has been barren and I have reached extreme old age?" The answer came: "Thus it will be; your Lord says, \'It is easy for Me.\'" Zakariyya asked for a sign to reassure his heart, and his sign was that he would not speak to people for three consecutive nights while being sound in body, with no defect or illness. So he came out to his people from the prayer chamber, signaling to them with his hand only that they should exalt Allah morning and evening, unable to speak despite being in perfect health. Yahya was born, and Allah gave him wisdom while he was still a young boy, and made him dutiful and kind to his parents; he was not a disobedient tyrant. So peace from Allah was upon him the day he was born, the day he dies, and the day he is raised alive.',
    source: 'Surah Maryam: 2–15 and Surah Al Imran: 37–41',
  ),
  'prophet-22': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Hud and the People of \'Ad',
    body:
        'Allah sent Hud, peace be upon him, to the people of \'Ad. They were a people of tremendous physical strength and great buildings, who erected towering palaces and impregnable fortresses, thinking that these would make them live forever on earth. Hud called them to worship Allah alone and reminded them of His blessings upon them: "And remember when He made you successors after the people of Nuh (Noah) and increased you in stature extensively. So remember the favors of Allah that you might succeed." But they were arrogant and mocked his call with open contempt: "Have you come to delude us away from our gods? Then bring us what you promise us, if you should be of the truthful!" Hud denounced them for building towers and palaces in vain, with no real need other than showing off: "Do you construct on every elevation a sign, amusing yourselves, and take for yourselves palaces and fortresses that you might abide eternally?" He warned them very clearly that a great punishment awaited them if they persisted in their tyranny, and they replied with the arrogance of the physical strength that had deluded them: "Who is greater than us in strength?" They forgot that Allah, who created them, is far greater than them in strength, beyond any comparison. When they persisted in their stubbornness, one day they saw a thick cloud heading toward their valleys, and they rejoiced at it, thinking it was the long-awaited rain after a drought. Hud said to them in warning: "Rather, it is that for which you were impatient: a wind, within it a painful punishment, destroying everything by command of its Lord." So Allah sent upon them a screaming, violent wind, which He imposed upon them for seven nights and eight days in succession. It tore mighty men from the ground and threw them down as if they were the trunks of hollow palm trees, eaten away from within, until the people of \'Ad were wiped out completely, down to the last of them. None of them remained except Hud and those who believed with him. Their strength and their palaces availed them nothing against the command of Allah.',
    source:
        'Surah Al-A\'raf: 65–72, Surah Hud: 50–60, and Surah Al-Haqqah: 6–8',
  ),
  // ===== Animals =====
  'animal-1': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Ant Who Warned Her People',
    body:
        'Sulayman (Solomon), peace be upon him, was traveling in a great procession that included his soldiers from among humans, jinn, and birds, lined up in precise order. On their way, they passed through a valley full of ants. An ant saw from afar that vast army approaching with its many hooves and feet, and she realized the danger to her small people, who might not sense the enormity of what was coming toward them. So she cried out among them at the top of her voice: "O ants, enter your dwellings that you not be crushed by Sulayman and his soldiers while they perceive not." Notice the good opinion she had of Sulayman and his soldiers: she did not say that they would crush them deliberately and out of malice, but said "while they perceive not," excusing them in advance should it happen. Allah had granted Sulayman the miracle of understanding the language of animals and birds, so he heard her words perfectly clearly, despite her tiny size and her faint voice, which others could barely hear. Sulayman smiled, amused at the cleverness of that ant and the way she looked after her people. He was not too proud to listen to the words of a creature so tiny beside his great kingdom; rather, he turned at once to his Lord in supplication and gratitude: "My Lord, enable me to be grateful for Your favor which You have bestowed upon me and upon my parents and to do righteousness of which You approve. And admit me by Your mercy into the ranks of Your righteous servants." In this simple moment lies a great lesson in humility: Allah\'s blessings upon a servant, however great they may be, should lead him to more gratitude, not to arrogance and pride.',
    source: 'Surah An-Naml: 18–19',
  ),
  'animal-2': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The She-Camel of Salih',
    body:
        'The people of Thamud were a mighty people who carved houses out of the mountains. Allah sent Salih, peace be upon him, to them, calling them to worship Him alone and abandon the worship of idols. They demanded from him a miraculous sign to prove his truthfulness, so Allah brought forth for them, from a solid rock, a she-camel of enormous size, a clear sign before all their eyes. Salih gave them two conditions: that they not harm her, and that they leave her a whole day to drink alone from the water of the well, with the people drinking on another day. He warned them in the strongest terms that if they hamstrung her, a swift and painful punishment would strike them. The people lived with this she-camel for a time, but some of them began to grow weary of her. One of them, together with some of the most wretched of the people, was tempted by his own soul to get rid of her. So they gathered and hamstrung her, killing her in a brutal way, in open defiance of their prophet\'s command. When Salih learned what they had done, he warned them of an imminent punishment: "Enjoy yourselves in your homes for three days. That is a promise not to be denied." The three days passed, with people\'s hearts torn between fear and mockery, until on the fourth day a mighty blast came upon them from the sky, and the earth shook beneath them. They lay fallen prone in their homes. They all perished in a single moment, old and young alike, because they did not keep their covenant with their prophet in a simple matter: to let a she-camel drink on one day of the week.',
    source:
        'Surah Al-A\'raf: 73–79, Surah Ash-Shams: 11–15, and Surah Hud: 61–68',
  ),
  'animal-3': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Dog of the People of the Cave',
    body:
        'There were some young believers in a town whose people worshipped idols under the rule of a tyrant king who forced people to worship him. These young men believed in Allah in secret and feared persecution and death, so they decided to flee with their faith to a remote cave in the mountain that no one knew of, hoping that Allah would make their affair easy for them. When they set out fleeing, their dog, which they had been raising, followed them and would not leave them, even though it did not understand the danger of the situation as they did. It stretched out its forelegs at the entrance of the cave, as if guarding them, standing as a faithful guard at their door. Allah cast upon them a deep sleep that lasted many long years. He preserved them in it by turning their bodies to the right and to the left from time to time, so that their bodies would not waste away from lying against the ground, and He preserved the dog with them in the same position at the entrance. Three hundred and nine lunar years passed while they slept, without their sensing the passage of time. Then Allah awakened them from their sleep. Some of them thought they had slept only a day or part of a day, until they sent one of them to buy food with their old silver coins. The people of the city discovered that their coins were very old and had not been used for a long time, and so they came to know of the miracle of their awakening after these long centuries. What is notable in the story is that the dog is mentioned respectfully in the Qur\'an, and its presence with them is not looked down upon; rather, it became a symbol of loyal companionship and good fellowship, so much so that it is said to be one of the few animals mentioned favorably in the Noble Qur\'an.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Kahf: 9–26',
  ),
  'animal-4': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Man Who Gave Water to a Dog',
    body:
        'The Prophet ﷺ told his Companions the story of a man from the nations of old who was walking along a long road. He became so thirsty that he nearly perished from it. He found a well on his way, so he climbed down into it and drank until his thirst was quenched. When he came out, he found a dog panting from severe thirst, so thirsty that it was eating the moist earth. The man said to himself: "This dog has become as thirsty as I was." His heart softened toward the poor animal, even though he did not know it and it was no concern of his, and he decided not to leave a creature suffering in front of him when he was able to help it. The man went down into the well once again, which required extra effort and time from him. He filled his leather sock, his footwear, with water, then held it in his mouth so that he could climb out of the well using his hands. He climbed up with difficulty until he reached the dog and gave it water until its thirst was quenched. Allah appreciated this merciful deed of his, even though it was done not for a human being but for an animal from which no favor in return could be expected, and Allah forgave his sins because of this deed. When the Companions asked the Prophet ﷺ in amazement: "O Messenger of Allah, is there a reward for us even in animals?" he ﷺ answered them with a great principle that covers every act of mercy a person shows: "In every creature with a moist liver there is a reward," meaning that there is a reward for whoever does good to any living creature, whether human or animal.',
    source: 'Sahih al-Bukhari and Muslim',
  ),
  'animal-5': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Woman Who Tormented a Cat',
    body:
        'In contrast to the story of the man who showed mercy to the dog, the Prophet ﷺ warned against hardness of heart toward animals. He told the story of a woman from the nations of the past who entered the Fire because of a cat — not because she killed it outright, but because she locked it up and tied it without food or drink until it died of hunger and thirst. Nor was that all: she even prevented it from eating the insects of the earth to keep itself alive. So her crime was twofold: imprisoning the animal and, at the same time, depriving it of the most basic means of survival. The hadith does not mention why she locked up the cat, but its main lesson is clear: mercy toward animals is a religious duty, and neglecting it carries a clear punishment, just as kindness toward them carries a great reward. In some narrations of the hadith, this story comes directly alongside the story of the man who gave water to the dog, so that the Prophet ﷺ placed before his companions two opposite situations: one man\'s mercy brought him into Paradise, and one woman\'s cruelty brought her into the Fire — even though both of them were dealing with an animal that had no power or strength of its own.',
    source: 'Sahih al-Bukhari and Muslim',
  ),
  'animal-6': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Crow That Taught the Son of Adam',
    body:
        'Adam, peace be upon him, had two sons, Qabil (Cain) and Habil (Abel). Each of them offered a sacrifice to Allah as a test of his sincerity. Allah accepted the offering of Habil, who was God-fearing, and did not accept the offering of Qabil, who had offered the worst of what he had. Qabil was filled with envy and anger toward his brother and threatened him, saying: "I will surely kill you." Habil answered him with the wisdom of a believer who does not want to shed blood: "Indeed, Allah only accepts from the righteous. If you should raise your hand against me to kill me, I shall not raise my hand against you to kill you. Indeed, I fear Allah, Lord of the worlds." But envy blinded Qabil\'s heart, and he killed his brother with his own hand, becoming the first murderer in human history; it was the first murder ever committed on the face of the earth. After the crime, Qabil stood over his brother\'s body, bewildered, not knowing what to do with it, for this was the first time a human being had ever seen a dead body, and he did not know how to bury it. So Allah sent a crow scratching at the ground with its claws and beak right before Qabil\'s eyes, to show him how to dig a hole in which to hide his brother\'s body. There was another crow that had been killed by a crow like itself, and the living crow had come to bury its dead companion. When Qabil saw this scene, he deeply regretted his crime and his helplessness even to bury his brother, and he said, admitting his wrong to himself: "O woe to me! Have I failed to be like this crow and hide the body of my brother?" And he became one of the regretful — but only after it was too late.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Ma\'idah: 27–31',
  ),
  'animal-7': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Uzair\'s Donkey, Brought Back to Life',
    body:
        'A righteous man — said to have been Uzair (Ezra), peace be upon him — passed by a town that had been utterly ruined: its roofs had fallen in upon its walls, and its people had perished long ago, until no trace of life remained in it. He stopped to reflect on this ruin and wondered to himself in amazement: "How will Allah bring this to life after its death?" This was not a question of doubt about Allah\'s power, but a natural wonder at the overwhelming scene before him. So Allah caused him to die in that very spot for a full hundred years, then brought him back to life and asked him: "How long have you remained?" Not knowing what had really happened, he answered: "I have remained a day or part of a day," thinking that his sleep had not lasted long. He was told: "Rather, you have remained one hundred years. Look at your food and your drink that were with you; they have not changed with time" — that is, they had not changed or spoiled even though a whole century had passed — "and look at your donkey." And here was the great miracle: he looked at the bones of his donkey, which had decayed and scattered over the long years, and he saw with his own eyes how Allah raised them from their places and arranged them bone upon bone, then clothed them with flesh, then breathed life into it, until the donkey stood alive before him, braying as if nothing had happened. When all of this had become perfectly clear to him before his very eyes, not the slightest doubt remained in him, and he said, acknowledging the perfection of Allah\'s power: "I know that Allah is over all things competent."',
    source: 'Surah Al-Baqarah: 259',
  ),
  'animal-8': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Creature That Revealed Sulayman\'s Death',
    body:
        'Allah made armies of jinn subservient to Sulayman (Solomon), peace be upon him, working for him at heavy tasks that humans could not manage: they built for him great prayer chambers, statues, bowls as large as reservoirs, and cauldrons so huge they stayed fixed in place. The jinn claimed that they knew the unseen — a false claim by which they wanted to set themselves above humans. The appointed time of Sulayman\'s death came, peace be upon him, while he was standing, leaning on his staff, watching the jinn at work as usual. Allah took his soul while he was in this state, but his noble body remained upright, supported by his staff, so neither the jinn nor the people around him realized right away that he had died. They thought he was still alive, overseeing their work with his usual watchfulness, and so they carried on with the hard labor for many long months, believing he was watching them from over his staff. The truth of his death was only revealed when a small creature of the earth — the termite, an ant-like insect that feeds on wood — gnawed at his staff very slowly, day after day, until it had eaten away its inside completely without anyone noticing. At last the staff broke under the weight of the body, and the body of Sulayman, peace be upon him, fell to the ground. Only then did the jinn know with complete certainty that he had died a long time before, and that they had kept toiling at their hard forced labor all those months for nothing — which would not have happened if they had truly known the unseen as they claimed. In this event, which seems small on the surface — the work of a tiny insect that can hardly be seen — there is a great lesson that shows people the falsehood of the jinn\'s claim to know the unseen, and that Allah alone knows what is hidden from sight. Had the jinn truly known the unseen, they would not have remained in humiliating punishment, working for a dead man who was giving them no orders.',
    source: 'Surah Saba: 14',
  ),
  // ===== The Righteous =====
  'righteous-1': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Uways al-Qarani',
    body:
        'In a well-known hadith, the Prophet ﷺ told his companions that after him there would come a man from the people of Yemen named Uways ibn Amir, from the tribe of Qarn, and he gave his companions precise signs by which they would recognize him: he had white patches on his skin — leprosy — and he prayed to Allah, who removed it all except for a small spot the size of a dirham, which remained on purpose as a sign by which he could be identified. He had an elderly mother whom he honored and served with true devotion, and he was described as a man who, if he swore an oath by Allah, Allah would fulfill it, because of how sincere and truthful he was with Him. The Prophet ﷺ specifically instructed the caliph Umar ibn al-Khattab to ask him to seek forgiveness for him if he met him, because his supplication was answered. Years passed, until a delegation from Yemen came to Umar ibn al-Khattab in Madinah. As was his habit whenever a delegation arrived from Yemen, Umar asked: "Is Uways ibn Amir among you?" — until at last he found him among the new arrivals. Umar asked him about his name, his tribe and his circumstances, and he answered with exactly the same descriptions the Prophet ﷺ had mentioned: from Murad, then from Qarn; he had a mother whom he served; and he had had leprosy, and he prayed to Allah and He removed it except for a spot the size of a dirham. Umar was certain that this was precisely the man meant, and so, with the humility of a caliph standing before a man unknown to ordinary people, he asked him: "Seek forgiveness for me." Uways sought forgiveness for him, even though Uways himself had never seen the Prophet ﷺ in his life or met him. Yet the faith in his heart and his sincerity with Allah had reached such a great level that it made him the very best of the Successors (Tabi\'in). This is a lesson that a person\'s standing with Allah does not come from being well known, but from sincerity of heart and devotion in one\'s deeds.',
    source: 'Sahih Muslim',
  ),
  'righteous-2': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Three Men in the Cave',
    body:
        'Three men set out on a journey long ago. Rain caught them, so they took shelter in a cave in the mountain. Then a huge rock rolled down from the top of the mountain and blocked the mouth of the cave completely. They were trapped in the darkness, unable to move or get out, and they were certain that if they stayed like this they would die of hunger and thirst inside that dark prison. In a moment of despair, they said to one another: "By Allah, nothing will save you from this rock except calling upon Allah by your righteous deeds." So the first of them began to tell his story: "O Allah, I had two elderly parents, and I never gave anyone in my family or my flock to drink before them. One day I went far away in search of pasture, and I did not come back to them until they had fallen asleep. I milked their milk for them and found them asleep. I did not want to wake them, and I did not want to give my family or my flock to drink before them, so I stayed waiting with the cup in my hand until dawn broke, while the little children were crying from hunger at my feet. O Allah, if I did that seeking Your Face, then relieve us of what we are in." The rock moved a little, but they still could not get out. The second said: "O Allah, I had a cousin whom I loved with the most intense love. I wanted her for myself, but she refused, until a year of severe drought struck her and she came to me in need. I gave her one hundred and twenty dinars on condition that she give herself to me. When I had her in my power, she said: \'Fear Allah, and do not break the seal except by its right.\' So I left her out of fear of You, despite how intensely I loved her, and I left her the money I had given her. O Allah, if I did that seeking Your Face, then relieve us of what we are in." The rock moved further, but they were still unable to get out. The third said: "O Allah, I hired some workers and gave them their wages, except for one man who left his wage and went away. I invested his wage in trade for him until it grew and became wealth, cattle, sheep and slaves. Then, many years later, he came to me asking for what was his, and I said to him: \'All that you see of my wealth is yours.\' He took all of it and left none of it behind. O Allah, if I did that seeking Your Face, then relieve us of what we are in." After this last supplication the rock moved away completely, and the three came out walking free in the sunlight, thanks to the sincerity of their deeds, which they had done purely for Allah alone without anyone among the people knowing about them.',
    source: 'Sahih al-Bukhari and Muslim',
  ),
  'righteous-3': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz and His Justice',
    body:
        'Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz was one of the wealthiest young men of the Umayyad family, living the life of luxury that was usual for the princes of his time, until the caliphate came to him unexpectedly after the death of the caliph Sulayman ibn Abd al-Malik, who had chosen him as his successor even though he was not his son. On the first day of his caliphate, he went out to the people and stood addressing them in tears. He said that he had no desire for this position and no eagerness for it, and he asked the people to pledge allegiance to someone else if they wished; but the people pledged allegiance to him willingly, because they knew his piety. The first thing he did after taking office was to gather everything that he and his family possessed of property and wealth that had not come to them by lawful right, and to return it in full to the public treasury of the Muslims — even the ring he used to wear was sold and its price handed over to the public treasury. His family\'s condition changed from immense wealth to a simple life of bare sufficiency. He cared about justice among all his subjects, without distinguishing between Muslim and non-Muslim, and he wrote to his governors in every province ordering them to lift injustices from the people and return rights to their owners. It was even said that poverty almost disappeared entirely in his time because there was so much justice and so little oppression, and people would search for someone who deserved zakah but could not find a poor person who would accept it. His caliphate lasted only two and a half years; he died after that while still a young man, but his just legacy remained engraved in the history of Islam, until he came to be counted as the fifth Rightly Guided Caliph after the first four caliphs.',
    source:
        'Books of history and biography — e.g. Sirat Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz by Ibn al-Jawzi',
  ),
  'righteous-4': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Piety of al-Hasan al-Basri',
    body:
        'Al-Hasan al-Basri was one of the most famous scholars of the Successors (Tabi\'in), among the most pious of them and the wisest in giving admonition, so much so that when he spoke, the people around him would weep because of his sincerity and how deeply he himself was moved by what he said. One day a man asked him: "How are you this morning, O Abu Sa\'id?" He gave him a wise answer that has never been forgotten: "We wake in the morning and reach the evening with our appointed time in the hands of another. Whoever wants to see death alive, let him look at us. We have become guests in this world, and our wealth in it is on loan; the guest will depart, and the loan will be returned." He feared vanity and self-admiration in himself more than he feared sins in others, and he would say to his companions: "I have never seen anything without seeing Allah as greater than it" — meaning that he kept the greatness of Allah in mind in every scene he saw. Among his most famous sayings about death: "I have never seen a certainty in which there is no doubt that looks more like a doubt in which there is no certainty than death. People are certain of death, yet they act like those who are not certain of it." Al-Hasan saw that being heedless of death while being certain of it is one of the strangest contradictions in human behavior. He lived detached from worldly life, even though caliphs and governors would seek him out to hear his admonitions and would defer to his opinion in many matters — yet he never used this standing to gain status or wealth.',
    source: 'Biographical works — e.g. Hilyat al-Awliya by Abu Nu\'aym',
  ),
  'righteous-5': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Steadfastness of Sa\'id ibn al-Musayyib',
    body:
        'Sa\'id ibn al-Musayyib was the leader of the Successors (Tabi\'in) in his time and the most knowledgeable of them about the judgments and rulings of the companions, so much so that his knowledge was proverbial in every region, and students of knowledge came to him from everywhere. The ruler of his time offered him the chance to marry his daughter to the caliph\'s son — an offer many people dreamed of, for the prestige and standing it would bring. Sa\'id refused this offer without hesitation, because he did not want his knowledge or his closeness to people to become a means of gaining worldly benefits or becoming related by marriage to those in power. Instead, he married his daughter to one of his poor students who was devoted to knowledge, honoring him for his knowledge and piety, not for wealth or status. Sa\'id faced severe pressure from some governors who wanted him to give a particular pledge of allegiance or to give up an opinion he believed to be true. He refused all of that and endured severe harm and beatings for his stance, but he did not move from his position even a fingertip\'s breadth, and he would repeat his famous saying: "I will not sell my religion for anyone\'s worldly gain." Through this steadfastness he became a byword for firmness upon the truth, and he remained a teacher whose example was followed in jurisprudence and piety for many long centuries after his death.',
    source: 'Biographical works — e.g. Siyar A\'lam al-Nubala by al-Dhahabi',
  ),
  'righteous-6': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Imam Ahmad ibn Hanbal\'s Steadfastness in the Trial',
    body:
        'In the time of the caliph al-Ma\'mun, and after him al-Mu\'tasim, the state adopted a view in matters of creed claiming that the Qur\'an was created, and it forced scholars to profess it under threat of punishment. Many scholars agreed out of fear of prison or death — except Imam Ahmad ibn Hanbal, who stood alone in rejecting this claim, holding firmly to the creed of the early generations (the Salaf) that he had learned, and refusing to say something for which there was no evidence from the Book or the Sunnah, whatever it cost him. Imam Ahmad was imprisoned for many years and flogged severely in front of people until he lost consciousness more than once, yet he still did not move from his position, saying to those around him: "How can I say what neither Allah, nor His Messenger, nor the companions before me said?" When he was given the choice between agreeing with them and being released and honored, or holding on to his view and being tortured, he chose torture over going against what he believed to be true. The trial lasted many years, through the reigns of three caliphs, until the situation finally changed and the view of Ahl al-Sunnah prevailed — the view that Imam Ahmad had held firmly almost alone among the senior scholars. He became an everlasting example that one man\'s steadfastness upon the truth can protect the creed of an entire Ummah from going astray.',
    source:
        'Biographical and historical works — e.g. Siyar A\'lam al-Nubala by al-Dhahabi',
  ),
  'righteous-7': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Repentance of al-Fudayl ibn Iyad',
    body:
        'Before his repentance, al-Fudayl ibn Iyad was a notorious highway robber who terrified travelers between Khurasan and Sarakhs, known for his cruelty and his boldness in robbing and plundering. The story of his remarkable repentance began one night when he was climbing a wall to get to a woman he desired unlawfully. While he was on top of the wall, he heard a reciter in a neighboring house reciting a verse from the Book of Allah: "Has the time not come for those who have believed that their hearts should become humbly submissive at the remembrance of Allah?" The verse struck him to the core, and he immediately said to himself, while still on top of the wall: "Yes, my Lord, the time has come." He climbed down from the wall at once, abandoning what he had intended to do, and headed for a deserted ruin to spend the night there. By chance, he found in it a group of travelers talking fearfully among themselves, saying: "Let us set off at dawn, when Fudayl the highway robber is asleep, so that he does not block our way." Hearing his own name, and seeing how afraid people were of him at that very moment, made his heart even more broken and full of regret. He said to himself: "I spend my nights in sin and terrify Muslim travelers who are out seeking their livelihood. By Allah, He brought me to this place to guide me!" From that night on he left his old life completely and devoted himself to worship and seeking knowledge, until he became one of the most eminent scholars of his time and one of the most devoted in worship and piety. He was even given the title "the Worshipper of the Two Sanctuaries" because of how long he stayed and worshipped in Makkah and Madinah. And so he was transformed from a feared highway robber into a guide whose example is followed in asceticism and God-consciousness.',
    source: 'Biographical works — e.g. Hilyat al-Awliya by Abu Nu\'aym',
  ),
  'righteous-8': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Rabi\'a al-Adawiyya and the Love of Allah',
    body:
        'Rabi\'a al-Adawiyya was born into a poor family in Basra and was orphaned young, until she fell into slavery and was sold as a servant to a man. One night her master noticed a light illuminating the whole house while she was prostrating in prayer in the depths of the night. He realized that she was not like other servants, and he freed her at once, out of respect for the sincerity of her worship that he had seen. After that, Rabi\'a devoted herself to the worship of her Lord alone and turned down many marriage proposals from wealthy, pious men of high standing. She would say that she found no room in her heart for anything but the love of her Lord, which had taken her away from everything else in the world. Rabi\'a became famous for teaching people that true worship should be pure love for Allah, not merely out of desire for His Paradise or fear of His Fire. She used to make her famous supplication: "O Allah, if I worship You out of fear of Your Fire, then burn me in it; and if I worship You out of desire for Your Paradise, then deny it to me; but if I worship You for Your own sake, then do not deny me Your everlasting beauty." She would spend the hours of the night weeping in her prayer, immersed in the remembrance of her Lord until she forgot the worldly matters around her. Her example became a foundation for later generations in understanding the meaning of complete sincerity in worship, free of any calculation or expectation of reward.',
    source: 'Biographical works — e.g. Sifat al-Safwa by Ibn al-Jawzi',
  ),
  // ===== Children =====
  'child-1': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Zamzam and Little Isma\'il',
    body:
        'The Prophet Ibrahim (Abraham) left his wife Hajar (Hagar) and her baby son Isma\'il (Ishmael) in a valley with no water and no plants, obeying the command of his Lord. Hajar asked him: "Did Allah command you to do this?" He said: "Yes." So she said calmly: "Then He will not abandon us." When the water they had with them ran out and Isma\'il cried from thirst, Hajar ran between the two hills of Safa and Marwah seven times, looking for any sign of water or someone who could help her, while Isma\'il cried and rubbed his little foot on the sand because he was so thirsty. Then Allah made a gushing spring of water burst out from under his foot. Hajar hurried to gather it, saying: "Zummi, zummi!" — which means "Gather together!" — and so it was named Zamzam from her words. This well, from which a young mother and her baby son drank in the empty desert, still flows with water today, thousands of years later, and millions of pilgrims performing Hajj and Umrah drink from it every year.',
    source: 'Sahih al-Bukhari (simplified for children)',
  ),
  'child-2': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Truthful Boy',
    body:
        'There was once a young boy who was learning from the king\'s sorcerer. Every day on his way, he would pass by a righteous monk, and he loved what the monk said, so he believed in Allah in secret. Allah gave him a wonderful blessing: the ability to pray for the sick to be healed, by Allah\'s permission — even a blind man who had not been able to see since birth was healed. When the unjust king found out about him, he was angry that the boy believed in a Lord other than the king, so he tried to kill him in many ways, but Allah protected him every time. In the end, the boy told the king of a way he could kill him: to gather the people in one place and shoot him with an arrow, saying: "In the name of Allah, the Lord of the boy." Even though this would lead to his death, he wanted all the people to declare their faith in his Lord before he died. The king did this, and the boy died, but all the people who witnessed the scene cried out together: "We believe in the Lord of the boy!" So the boy was victorious despite his death, because all of his people came to believe because of him.',
    source: 'Sahih Muslim (simplified for children)',
  ),
  'child-3': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Palm Trunk That Longed for the Prophet',
    body:
        'The Prophet ﷺ used to give the Friday sermon to the people while leaning against an old palm trunk in the mosque, because there was no pulpit yet. When his companions made him a beautiful wooden pulpit with steps, and the Prophet ﷺ left the old trunk and went up onto the pulpit, everyone in the mosque heard a strange sound: a sound of longing and weeping coming from that trunk, like the crying of a sad little child! The trunk was weeping in sorrow at being parted from the Prophet ﷺ, because it had grown used to him leaning on it. So the Prophet ﷺ came down from his pulpit at once and hugged the trunk with his blessed hands until it grew quiet and calm, like a mother hugging her crying child. And the Prophet ﷺ said to his companions: "If I had not hugged it, it would have kept on crying like this until the Day of Resurrection, in sorrow at being parted from me."',
    source: 'Sahih al-Bukhari (simplified for children)',
  ),
  'child-4': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Wolf That Spoke',
    body:
        'A shepherd was grazing his sheep in the open country when a wolf suddenly came, snatched one of the sheep and ran off with it. The shepherd chased after it quickly and managed to rescue the sheep from it. But the wolf did something that had never happened before: it stood on a high place and spoke in words the shepherd could understand, saying to him: "Do you not fear Allah? You are keeping from me provision that Allah provided for me!" The shepherd was very, very amazed — had anyone ever seen a wolf talking like a human before? He hurried off to tell people what had happened, and the news reached the Prophet ﷺ himself. He believed the story and did not find it strange, and he said that this is one of the signs that the end of time is approaching, and that Allah is able to do all things — even to make an animal speak by His permission, if He wills.',
    source: 'Sahih al-Bukhari (simplified for children)',
  ),
  'child-5': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Musa and the Righteous Man',
    body:
        'Musa (Moses), peace be upon him, wanted to learn from a righteous man to whom Allah had given special knowledge that no one else had. The man agreed to let Musa accompany him on one condition: that Musa would not ask him about anything he did until he explained it to him himself first. But Musa saw the man do some very strange things: first, he made a hole in a ship that had been carrying them for free, so Musa was amazed and questioned him despite his promise! Then he saw the man kill a young, innocent boy, and Musa could not hold himself back and questioned him again. The man said to him: "Did I not say that with me you would never be able to have patience?" Then, in a third town, the people refused to feed them even though they were hungry — and yet the man repaired a leaning wall that was about to fall down, without taking any payment! So Musa questioned him for the third time, and the man said: "This is the parting between me and you, but first I will tell you the wisdom behind everything I did." He told him that the ship belonged to poor people, and making a hole in it protected it from an unjust king who was seizing every sound ship by force. That the boy would have grown up to be wicked and would have harmed his believing parents, so Allah gave them in his place a child who was better than him in righteousness. And that the wall was hiding beneath it a treasure belonging to two young orphans, so Allah kept it safe for them until they grew up. Then Musa understood that every strange action had a great wisdom behind it, which he would never have known had the righteous man not explained it to him.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Kahf: 60–82 (simplified for children)',
  ),
  'child-6': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Story of Adam in Paradise',
    body:
        'Allah created our father Adam from clay, breathed the soul into him, and taught him the names of everything around him. Then He created for him a wife named Hawwa (Eve) and let them live in beautiful Paradise, and He said to them: "Eat from its fruits as you wish, and go near any place you like — except for one single tree: do not go near it." But Shaytan (Satan) — who had been Adam\'s enemy ever since he refused to bow down to him — came and quietly whispered to them. He swore to them falsely that he was a sincere adviser, and that this tree would make them angels who would live forever. Adam and Hawwa believed his oath and forgot their Lord\'s warning, so they ate from the tree, and at once they felt shame and regret. They hurried to say sorry to Allah and called upon Him with words He had taught them: "Our Lord, we have wronged ourselves, and if You do not forgive us and have mercy upon us, we will surely be among the losers." Allah accepted their repentance, because He is Forgiving and Merciful, but He sent them down to the earth to be the first two humans to live on it — and so that this would be a lesson for all people never to believe the whispers of Shaytan, however beautiful they may seem.',
    source:
        'Surah Al-Baqarah: 35–37 and Surah Ta-Ha: 115–123 (simplified for children)',
  ),
  // ===== Companions =====
  'companion-1': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Abu Bakr in the Cave',
    body:
        'When the Prophet ﷺ migrated from Makkah to Madinah, escaping the plot of the idolaters who had agreed to kill him in his bed, his closest friend, Abu Bakr as-Siddiq, accompanied him on this dangerous journey. They chose an unusual route, and first hid in the Cave of Thawr, south of Makkah — the exact opposite direction from Madinah, to mislead those searching for them — and stayed there for three full days. Quraysh sent out men skilled at tracking footprints to search for them in every direction, until some of them reached the very mouth of the cave and stood there talking. Abu Bakr heard their voices very close by and became extremely afraid — not for himself, but for the Prophet ﷺ — and he whispered to him: "O Messenger of Allah, if one of them were to look down at his feet, he would see us." The Prophet ﷺ answered him with the confidence of a believer who trusts his Lord: "O Abu Bakr, what do you think of two, the third of whom is Allah? Do not grieve; indeed Allah is with us." Allah revealed a verse of the Qur\'an about this very moment, immortalizing the tranquility He sent down upon the Prophet ﷺ at that critical time. Allah saved them both despite how dangerously close the enemies were, until the searchers went away without discovering the cave, so that the Prophet ﷺ and his companion could complete their migration to Madinah in safety.',
    source: 'Surah At-Tawbah: 40 — Sahih al-Bukhari',
  ),
  'companion-2': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Patience of Bilal ibn Rabah',
    body:
        'Bilal ibn Rabah was an Abyssinian slave who worked for Umayyah ibn Khalaf in Makkah. When he heard the call of Islam, he believed in it with all his heart at once. When his master learned that he had become Muslim, he was furious and decided to torture him to force him to abandon his religion. He would take him out at midday, when the sun blazed hottest on the sands of Makkah, and throw him on his back on the burning sand that almost scorched the skin. Then he would order a huge, heavy rock to be placed on his chest to increase his suffering and stop him from moving, and he kept saying to him: "You will stay like this until you die, or until you reject Muhammad and worship al-Lat and al-\'Uzza." But Bilal, in the middle of all this unbearable torment, kept repeating one steadfast word in a voice broken by the intensity of the pain: "One, One! One, One!" — declaring the Oneness of Allah despite all the torture, and refusing to bargain away his faith no matter how great the pain became. One day Abu Bakr as-Siddiq passed by and saw this painful sight. His heart was moved with pity for Bilal, so he went to his master and bargained with him to buy him for a large sum of money, then freed him for the sake of Allah as soon as he had bought him. After that, Bilal became one of the senior Companions, and the Prophet ﷺ chose him to be the first mu\'adhdhin (caller to prayer) in the history of Islam, with the beautiful voice he had been tortured for refusing to silence. On the day of the Conquest of Makkah, he even climbed on top of the Ka\'bah itself and called the adhan in front of the very Quraysh who had once tortured him — a testimony that true faith is stronger than any torment, however severe.',
    source: 'Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'companion-3': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Ali in the Prophet\'s ﷺ Bed',
    body:
        'When the leaders of Quraysh agreed to kill the Prophet ﷺ by a cunning scheme — having a young man from every tribe take part in the killing together, so that the responsibility for his blood would be spread among all the tribes and Banu Hashim would be unable to take revenge on all of them — Allah informed His Prophet ﷺ of this conspiracy before it was carried out. The assassins gathered at night around the door of the Prophet\'s ﷺ house, waiting for him to come out so they could attack him. The Prophet ﷺ asked his cousin Ali ibn Abi Talib, who was still a young man, to sleep in his bed, covered with his green cloak, to make those lying in wait outside believe that the Prophet ﷺ was still asleep in his house, while he himself slipped out among them through an opportunity Allah gave him, without being seen. Ali agreed at once, without the slightest hesitation, even though he fully understood how dangerous the situation was, for he knew that the drawn swords might come down on him at any moment, with them thinking he was the Prophet ﷺ asleep. Ali slept in that bed the whole night, hearing the whispers of the assassins at the door, steady-hearted and unafraid, seeking Allah\'s reward for this great sacrifice in protecting the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. In the morning, when the assassins burst into the room, they were stunned to see Ali come out from under the cover instead of the Prophet ﷺ, and they realized that the journey had begun long hours before them. At that moment, the Prophet ﷺ had already covered a safe distance on his way to the Cave of Thawr, and then on to Madinah.',
    source: 'Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'companion-4': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Uthman and the Well of Rumah',
    body:
        'After the Hijrah, the Muslims in Madinah suffered from a serious problem: a shortage of fresh drinking water, since most of the wells around the city were salty or not enough. There was one well with sweet, good water called the Well of Rumah, but it was the private property of a Jewish man who took advantage of people\'s need for it and sold a single bucket at a very high price, which was a heavy burden on the poor among the Muslims. The Prophet ﷺ gathered his Companions and strongly urged them, saying: "Who will buy the Well of Rumah and make it for the Muslims, and have Paradise in return for it?" Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him — one of the wealthy Companions, known for his generosity — hurried to the Jewish owner of the well and tried to buy all of it, but the man refused to sell it entirely because he knew its value. So Uthman suggested a compromise: he would buy only half of it, with one day for the Muslims and one day for the owner, taking turns. The man reluctantly agreed, and Uthman bought the half with a great deal of his own money. On the day set aside for the Muslims, people would draw enough water to last them two days, so no one needed to buy water on the other day anymore. In the end, the owner was forced to sell the remaining half as well, for a small price, because he could no longer find anyone to buy from him. Uthman made the whole well a waqf (endowment) purely for the sake of Allah, for all the Muslims to benefit from free of charge forever. This charity became one of the greatest endowments in the history of Islam, and the lands of this endowment still stand and are still being put to use to this day, more than fourteen centuries later.',
    source: 'Narrated by at-Tirmidhi and an-Nasa\'i',
  ),
  'companion-5': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Khalid ibn al-Walid, the Sword of Allah',
    body:
        'Before he became Muslim, Khalid ibn al-Walid was one of the fiercest horsemen of Quraysh and the most skilled in the arts of battle. He was even the one who led the idolaters at the Battle of Uhud and devised a clever military plan — circling around from behind the Archers\' Hill — that caused a painful setback for the Muslims, even though they had been winning at the start of the battle. But his heart began to change little by little as the years went by, as he saw the miracles of Islam and the light of the truth became clearer and clearer before him, until he finally decided to declare his Islam. He went to Madinah and accepted Islam in front of the Prophet ﷺ, who welcomed him with great joy and gave him the good news of a bright future serving the religion he had been fighting only the day before. From the moment he became Muslim, Khalid placed all his immense military experience in the service of Islam. The Prophet ﷺ put him in command of decisive battles and gave him the title that stayed with him forever: the Drawn Sword of Allah, in honor of his great strength in defending the religion. Khalid fought in more than a hundred battles in his life — before and after becoming Muslim — and was never once defeated in any of them, a military achievement that is rare in all of human history. Yet despite all this strength and courage on the battlefield, Khalid did not die a martyr as he had always wished and often said. Instead, he died in his bed of natural illness. Before his death he wept and said with sorrow: "I have taken part in so many battles, and there is not a hand-span of my body that does not bear a sword blow, a spear thrust, or an arrow wound — and here I am, dying in my bed as a donkey dies. May the eyes of cowards never sleep!"',
    source: 'Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'companion-6': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Sacrifice of Mus\'ab ibn Umayr',
    body:
        'Before Islam, Mus\'ab ibn Umayr lived the most comfortable life of all the young men of Makkah and wore the finest clothes. He was the son of a wealthy family who spoiled him and gave him every luxury in life, so much so that it was said he was the example people gave for good grooming and beautiful clothing. When he secretly became Muslim and his mother found out, she locked him up and tried by every means to force him to leave his new religion. He did not move even a fingertip\'s width from his faith, despite his family\'s heavy pressure. In the end, his mother cut him off from all the wealth she used to shower on him, and Mus\'ab suddenly went from a life of luxury to a life of extreme poverty, until he was wearing worn-out, patched clothes that barely covered his body. The Prophet ﷺ sent him to Madinah before the Hijrah to teach its people the Qur\'an and invite them to Islam, so he was the first ambassador of Islam in its history. He succeeded greatly, and many of the leaders of the Aws and Khazraj became Muslim through him, paving the way for the Prophet\'s ﷺ later migration. Mus\'ab was martyred at the Battle of Uhud while carrying the banner of the Muslims in his hand, defending it with all his strength, until his right hand was cut off. He then held it with his left hand, and his left hand was cut off too, so he hugged the banner with his upper arms until he was martyred. When they came to shroud him, they could not find enough cloth to cover him: if they covered his head, his feet showed, and if they covered his feet, his head showed. So the Prophet ﷺ ordered that his head be covered and that some idhkhir grass be placed over his feet. The Prophet ﷺ wept bitterly for him, remembering Mus\'ab\'s fine clothes in Makkah before Islam and comparing them with his state at the time of his martyrdom.',
    source: 'Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'companion-7': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Sumayyah, the First Martyr of Islam',
    body:
        'Sumayyah bint Khayyat was a woman who had neither a powerful tribe to protect her nor wealth to shield her. She was the mother of Ammar ibn Yasir, and one of the very first to believe in Islam in Makkah, at its most dangerous time. For this very reason, Abu Jahl and the chiefs of Quraysh chose her family — Sumayyah, her husband Yasir, and their son Ammar — to make a harsh example of them for others, since they had no one to defend them, unlike many of the early Muslims who were protected by their clans. They would be taken out onto the scorching sands of Makkah at midday and tortured for days on end in the most horrible ways, in an attempt to force them to turn back from their religion. The Prophet ﷺ would pass by them during that ordeal, unable to help them at that early stage, so he would comfort them with words that history has preserved: "Patience, O family of Yasir, for your promised meeting place is Paradise." Sumayyah endured unbearable torture without her faith moving even a fingertip\'s width, until Abu Jahl lost all patience with her astonishing steadfastness against every kind of torment, and he stabbed her fatally with a spear. She fell a martyr on the spot. Sumayyah thus became the first martyr in the entire history of Islam — a woman who seemed weak in the eyes of the world, with no status or power, yet her faith was stronger than all the tools of her torturers.',
    source: 'Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'companion-8': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Salman al-Farisi\'s Journey in Search of the Truth',
    body:
        'Salman was originally a Persian from a wealthy family. He was raised worshipping fire, but he was not convinced by it, so he began secretly searching for the true religion until he came to know some Christian monks. He left his family and his wealth and set out, moving from one monk to another more truthful and knowledgeable one each time one of them died, searching for complete guidance. The last monk he accompanied, before he died, told him the signs of a prophet who would soon appear in the land of the Arabs: he would migrate to a land between two harrahs — fields of volcanic rock — full of palm trees; he would accept gifts but would not eat from charity; and between his shoulders would be the Seal of Prophethood. Salman traveled toward that land, searching, but a group of Bedouins deceived him on the way and sold him as a slave, until he ended up enslaved in Madinah itself — the very land he had been searching for, without realizing he had already reached it. When the Prophet ﷺ migrated to Madinah, Salman tested him with all three signs, and they came true before his eyes one after another. He wanted to enter Islam at once, but he was still a slave, so the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions helped him free himself: they agreed with his master that a large number of date palms would be planted for him as the price of Salman\'s freedom. They did so, and Salman became free, and one of the Companions dearest to the heart of the Prophet ﷺ. He was also the one whose idea later saved Madinah at the Battle of the Trench.',
    source: 'Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'companion-9': StoryTranslation(
    title:
        'Khadijah, May Allah Be Pleased with Her, at the Start of Revelation',
    body:
        'Khadijah bint Khuwaylid was a respected businesswoman in Makkah. Before the prophethood, she admired the honesty and trustworthiness of Muhammad ﷺ in managing her trade, so she married him. When revelation came to him for the first time in the Cave of Hira, and Jibril (Gabriel) held him tightly and commanded him, "Read!", the Prophet ﷺ was deeply frightened by the overwhelming experience. He returned home trembling and saying, "Cover me, cover me!", fearing for himself because of what he had seen. Khadijah did not hesitate for a single moment to reassure him. Instead, she said words that have been preserved in the books of the Sirah: "No, by Allah, Allah will never disgrace you. You keep ties with your relatives, you carry the burdens of the weak, you help the destitute, you honor your guests, and you support people through the hardships that befall them." Then she immediately took him to her cousin Waraqah ibn Nawfal, an old man who followed the Christian faith and knew the earlier scriptures. Waraqah told him that the one who had come to him was the great Namus (the angel of revelation) who used to come to Musa (Moses), peace be upon him, and that he was the prophet of this nation, and he warned him that his people would turn against him when he proclaimed his message openly. Khadijah was the very first human being to believe in Islam, and she spent all her wealth supporting the call during the harshest years of the siege and boycott. The Prophet ﷺ never forgot her loyalty. Even many years after her death, whenever he saw her sister, he would remember her with love, and he would honor those who had known her.',
    source:
        'Sahih al-Bukhari and Muslim — Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'companion-10': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'Zayd ibn Harithah Chooses the Prophet ﷺ over His Father',
    body:
        'Zayd ibn Harithah was captured as a young boy in a raid and sold as a slave, until he came into the possession of Khadijah, may Allah be pleased with her, who later gave him as a gift to the Prophet ﷺ before the prophethood. The Prophet freed him and adopted him, treating him as his own son. Many years later, Zayd\'s father and uncle finally managed to reach Makkah after an exhausting search for him, bringing a large ransom to get him back, expecting that he would rush to return to his real family and the freedom he had lost. But the Prophet ﷺ, with his noble character, said to his father: "Call him and let him choose. If he chooses you, he is yours without any ransom. And if he chooses me, then by Allah, I am not one to prefer anyone over someone who has chosen me." They brought Zayd and gave him the choice between his father and uncle on one side, and staying with the Prophet ﷺ on the other. No one doubted that he would choose his family and his freedom, but Zayd surprised everyone by saying: "I would never choose anyone over you. To me, you are like a father and an uncle together." His father was shocked and tried to understand how his son could prefer slavery over freedom and family, so Zayd explained that he had seen something in this man that made him choose him of his own free will. The Prophet ﷺ was moved by this deep loyalty, so he immediately took him to the Ka\'bah and announced before the people: "Bear witness that Zayd is my son; he inherits from me and I inherit from him." So he came to be called Zayd ibn Muhammad, until the Qur\'an was later revealed correcting the understanding of adoption, and his name returned to Zayd ibn Harithah. But his place in the heart of the Prophet ﷺ remained just as it was, and he is the only Companion whose name is mentioned explicitly in the Noble Qur\'an.',
    source:
        'Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah) — Surah Al-Ahzab: 4-5 and 37',
  ),
  // ===== Battles =====
  'battle-1': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Great Battle of Badr',
    body:
        'This was the first major military confrontation between the Muslims and Quraysh. It began when the Prophet ﷺ set out to intercept a Quraysh trade caravan returning from Syria, led by Abu Sufyan. But Abu Sufyan learned of this, changed his caravan\'s route and escaped with it, and at the same time sent a messenger to Quraysh begging for urgent help. Quraysh marched out with a huge, heavily armed army of more than a thousand fighters, while the Prophet ﷺ had only a little over three hundred and ten men, most of them without armor or enough horses — they even had to take turns riding the few camels they had. When the two armies drew near each other at the wells of Badr, the Prophet ﷺ went into his tent and prayed to his Lord with great earnestness, raising his hands until his cloak fell from his shoulders, saying: "O Allah, if this small band is destroyed, You will not be worshipped on earth." Abu Bakr as-Siddiq reassured him: "O Messenger of Allah, enough of your pleading with your Lord, for He will surely fulfill what He has promised you." Allah answered his prayer and sent down the angels to fight alongside the believers and make their feet firm. The battle took place, and the Muslims won an overwhelming victory despite their small numbers and few weapons. Seventy of the chiefs of Quraysh who used to torture the Muslims in Makkah were killed, among them Abu Jahl himself, and another seventy were taken captive. This day was called the Day of al-Furqan (the Criterion), because it separated truth from falsehood with complete clarity, and it made the young Islamic state in Madinah stand firm before the greatest Arab power of that time.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Anfal — Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'battle-2': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Battle of Uhud',
    body:
        'After its humiliating defeat at Badr, Quraysh gathered its forces and marched out with an army of three thousand fighters seeking revenge. The Prophet ﷺ went out with a thousand of his Companions to meet them at Mount Uhud, but the head of the hypocrites, Abdullah ibn Ubayy, withdrew with a third of the army halfway there, angry because his opinion had not been followed. Only seven hundred fighters remained with the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ made a careful plan: he ordered fifty skilled archers to take position on a small hill behind the army, and gave them a firm command that left no room for their own judgment: "Do not leave your place no matter what you see, whether we win or lose, until I send for you." The battle began, and the ranks of Quraysh broke before the Muslims\' attack, and they turned and fled. The archers thought the battle had ended in a decisive victory, so they came down from the hill racing to collect the spoils, despite the warnings of their commander, Abdullah ibn Jubayr, who reminded them of the Prophet\'s ﷺ instruction — but only a few listened to him. Khalid ibn al-Walid — who at that time was still an idolater fighting on the side of Quraysh — took advantage of this gap that had suddenly opened. He circled around behind the hill with his horsemen and took the Muslims by surprise from behind while they were busy with the spoils. The balance of the battle suddenly turned, the ranks became mixed up, and the Prophet ﷺ himself was wounded in his blessed face and his front tooth was broken. Seventy of the best Companions were martyred, among them his uncle Hamzah, the Lion of Allah. This battle was a great and powerful lesson about the danger of disobeying the commands of the leadership, even at a moment of apparent victory, and verses of the Qur\'an were revealed about it, gently rebuking those who disobeyed the order and strengthening the hearts of the believers.',
    source:
        'Surah Aal-Imran: 121-175 — Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'battle-3': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Battle of the Trench',
    body:
        'In this battle, the largest force ever to fight the Muslims up to that time gathered together. The tribes of Quraysh and Ghatafan united with the Jews of Banu an-Nadir, who had stirred up this alliance after being expelled from Madinah, until the attacking Confederates numbered about ten thousand fighters, intending to wipe out the Muslims once and for all and uproot their young state completely. When the Prophet ﷺ learned how great the coming danger was, he consulted his Companions on how to defend Madinah. Salman al-Farisi — who had known this defensive method in Persia, and which had never been known among the Arabs before — suggested that they dig a deep, wide trench around the exposed side of Madinah from which the enemy could easily attack. The Prophet ﷺ liked the idea, and all the Muslims worked on digging the trench day and night without tiring. The Prophet ﷺ worked with his own blessed hands alongside them, carrying earth just like them and sharing with them the bitter cold and gnawing hunger, until they completed this enormous work in just a few days, despite how huge it was. When the Confederates arrived and saw the trench surrounding Madinah, they were completely taken by surprise, for they had never known this method in their wars before. They were unable to cross it with their horses, which were used to charging and retreating directly, so they besieged Madinah from a distance for many long days, during which the cold and hunger grew severe for the besieged Muslims inside. At the height of the siege, Allah sent against the Confederates a fierce, cold wind on a dark night, which tore up their tents, put out their fires, and overturned their cooking pots. Fear suddenly spread through their hearts for no visible reason, so their leader, Abu Sufyan, decided to withdraw with his entire army by night without any real fighting. The siege broke up, and the battle ended with Allah protecting the Muslims from the greatest danger they had ever faced.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Ahzab: 9-27 — Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'battle-4': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Treaty of Hudaybiyyah',
    body:
        'The Prophet ﷺ set out with more than one thousand four hundred of his Companions, heading to Makkah only to perform Umrah. They carried no weapons of war except the usual swords of a traveler, and they were wearing the garments of ihram as a clear declaration of their peaceful intentions. But Quraysh refused to let them enter Makkah, fearing for their prestige in front of the Arabs. So the Prophet ﷺ stopped at the area of al-Hudaybiyyah near Makkah, and after negotiations and an exchange of envoys, a peace treaty was agreed upon, whose terms would last ten full years with no war between the two sides. The Muslims were to return that year without performing Umrah, and come back the following year to perform Umrah for only three days. On the surface, the terms of the treaty seemed unfair to the Muslims, so much so that some of the senior Companions were angered by these conditions. Umar ibn al-Khattab objected strongly to the Prophet ﷺ, saying: "Are we not upon the truth and they upon falsehood? So why should we accept humiliation in our religion?" The Prophet ﷺ answered him with complete confidence: "I am the Messenger of Allah, and He will not abandon me." One of the most surprising terms of the treaty was that anyone from Quraysh who came to the Muslims as a Muslim would be sent back to them, while anyone who left the Muslims and went over to them would not be sent back — a condition that seemed completely unjust on the surface. But Allah described this treaty in the Qur\'an as a clear conquest, because the truth later became plain to see: the end of war between the two sides opened the door wide for people to mix with one another without fear, and in just the two years that followed, many times more people entered Islam than had entered it in all the nineteen years before, since the beginning of the call.',
    source: 'Surah Al-Fath — Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'battle-5': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Conquest of Makkah',
    body:
        'Quraysh broke the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah when it helped its allies attack the allies of the Muslims. So the Prophet ﷺ marched toward Makkah with a mighty army of ten thousand fighters — the largest army the Muslims had gathered up to that time — heading for the city that had unjustly driven him out only eight years earlier. The Prophet ﷺ kept the army\'s destination hidden even from many of his Companions to preserve the element of surprise, so he entered Makkah without Quraysh being able to organize any real resistance, and he ordered his commanders not to fight anyone except those who fought them. When the Prophet ﷺ finally stood at the door of the Ka\'bah, the people of Quraysh waited in great fear to learn their fate after everything they had done to him and his Companions over the years — torture, expulsion, siege, and wars. Everyone expected a terrible revenge to match the harm the Muslims had suffered. But the Prophet ﷺ surprised them all with one of the greatest acts of forgiveness in all of human history. He calmly asked them: "O people of Quraysh, what do you think I will do with you?" They answered, still knowing his generosity despite everything: "Good. You are a noble brother, and the son of a noble brother." So he said to them the immortal words: "Go, for you are free." He forgave everyone who had harmed him, tortured his Companions, fought him in earlier battles, and killed some of his relatives, without taking retribution from a single one of them, even though he had complete power to do so. This great forgiveness was a direct reason why all the people of Makkah entered Islam within a very short time after the conquest, for they had seen the character of this religion with their own eyes before hearing its teachings.',
    source: 'Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'battle-6': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Expedition of Bi\'r Ma\'unah — The Tragedy of the Qurra\'',
    body:
        'A man named Abu Bara\' Amir ibn Malik, the chief of a tribe from Najd, came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him to send some of his Companions with him to teach his people the Qur\'an and Islam, promising to protect them himself all along the way. For this mission, the Prophet ﷺ chose seventy of his finest Companions, who were known as the Qurra\' (the Reciters) because of how much of the Qur\'an they had memorized and how much they worshipped at night. Some of them worked during the day to earn a living, then gave it away in charity and spent their nights reciting and studying the Qur\'an, so they were called the People of the Suffah because they stayed close to the Prophet\'s ﷺ mosque for knowledge and worship. These seventy set out on a purely peaceful mission, with no intention of fighting, and traveled until they camped at a well called Ma\'unah. They sent one of their men carrying the Prophet\'s ﷺ letter to Amir ibn at-Tufayl — the leader of another tribe in the area — but this treacherous man killed the messenger without even reading the letter. Then he called on tribes from Najd to attack the rest of the Qurra\' treacherously while they felt safe and expected no betrayal, even though Abu Bara\' had promised to protect them. The treacherous tribes surrounded the Qurra\' on every side and took them completely by surprise. The Companions fought with rare courage despite the surprise and how few they were against such a large number, until almost all of them were martyred, to the last man, except for one or two men who miraculously survived to tell the Prophet ﷺ what had happened. The Prophet ﷺ grieved deeply over this tragedy, as he had not grieved over any other calamity in his life, and he even made qunut (a special supplication) in his prayer for a whole month, praying against the treacherous tribes who had killed his Companions while they were on a peaceful mission to teach people goodness. This event remains one of the most painful lessons in the Prophet\'s biography about the danger of treachery and breaking promises, and about the heavy price the early carriers of the Qur\'an paid to spread this religion.',
    source:
        'Sahih al-Bukhari and Muslim — Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'battle-7': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Battle of Mu\'tah',
    body:
        'The Prophet ﷺ sent a messenger carrying a letter to the ruler of Busra in Syria, inviting him to Islam, but one of the princes loyal to the Romans killed him. Killing messengers was one of the gravest, most unforgivable crimes among the Arabs, so the Prophet ﷺ prepared an army of only three thousand fighters to face the mighty Roman Empire. He appointed Zayd ibn Harithah as its commander and instructed: "If Zayd is killed, then Ja\'far ibn Abi Talib; and if Ja\'far is killed, then Abdullah ibn Rawahah." The Muslims met the army of the Romans and their allies at Mu\'tah, and it numbered more than a hundred thousand fighters — a huge difference the Muslims had never seen before. Zayd ibn Harithah was martyred first while fighting fiercely. Then Ja\'far ibn Abi Talib took the banner and fought until his right hand was cut off, so he held it with his left, and his left hand was cut off too. He then hugged the banner with his severed arms until he was martyred, and Allah gave him in exchange two wings with which he flies in Paradise, so from that day he was called Ja\'far at-Tayyar (Ja\'far the Flyer). After him, Abdullah ibn Rawahah took the banner and was also martyred, so that all three commanders the Prophet ﷺ had appointed were martyred on a single day. After them, Khalid ibn al-Walid took the banner — he had become Muslim only a few months earlier — and he showed rare military genius. He changed the formation of the army, moving some units to the back and others to the front, making the Romans believe that large reinforcements had reached the Muslims, so they pulled back a little. Khalid seized this opportunity and withdrew the entire Muslim army in perfect order, without further heavy losses. It was a great military achievement in itself for a small army to withdraw from facing a force many dozens of times larger without being wiped out.',
    source: 'Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
  'battle-8': StoryTranslation(
    title: 'The Battle of Hunayn',
    body:
        'Right after the Conquest of Makkah, the tribes of Hawazin and Thaqif gathered a large army, determined to confront the Muslims before their strength grew even greater after the conquest. The Prophet ﷺ marched out with an army of twelve thousand fighters, the largest Muslim army gathered up to that time, so that some of the Muslims, impressed by their great numbers, said: "We will not be defeated today because of small numbers." But this confidence in numbers alone nearly led to a serious setback. The army of Hawazin lay in ambush in the narrow passes of the valley of Hunayn and took the front of the Muslim army by surprise with heavy volleys of arrows at dawn. The front ranks were thrown into confusion and suddenly fell back, and the situation quickly collapsed, until for a few moments it seemed that the Muslims would be defeated. But the Prophet ﷺ stood completely firm in his place and did not move, riding his white mule and calling out in a loud voice: "I am the Prophet, no lie; I am the son of Abd al-Muttalib!" Then he ordered his uncle al-Abbas — who had a strong, booming voice — to call out to those retreating: "O companions of the Samurah tree!" — a reference to the Pledge of Ridwan. They heard his call, remembered their pledge, and were filled with zeal, and they came back racing from every side toward the Prophet ﷺ until they had gathered around him once again. The battle quickly turned in their favor after defeat had nearly happened: the army of Hawazin was crushingly defeated, and the Muslims gained great spoils of camels, sheep, and captives. Verses of the Qur\'an were revealed about this event, reminding the Muslims that victory comes from Allah alone and not from large numbers: "...and [on] the day of Hunayn, when your great number pleased you, but it did not avail you at all."',
    source:
        'Surah At-Tawbah: 25-26 — Books of the Prophet\'s biography (Sirah)',
  ),
};
